U_{m_{i}}.\ $Hence, there exists $1\leq i\leq n$ such that $\left[  z\right]  \in
U_{m_{i}}$ which implies that $Rm_{i}\subseteq Rz.\ $Thus minimal elements of
the family of $\left\{  Rm\right\}  _{m\in M^{\#}}$ is contained in
$\{Rm_{1},Rm_{2},\ldots,Rm_{n}\}$. For the converse, assume that the family
$\left\{  Rm\right\}  _{m\in M^{\#}}$ has only finitely many minimal elements
with respect to inclusion $\subseteq.$ Now, let $\{Rm_{1},Rm_{2},\ldots
,Rm_{n}\}$\ be the set of minimal elements of $\left\{  Rm\right\}  _{m\in
M^{\#}}.$\ Let $EC(M^{\#})=%
