\section{Introduction}
\label{sec1}
For time evolution problems, parallelization in the time direction has been a highly active research topic in recent years. This is because many time evolution problems cannot be quickly computed to obtain the desired answers on modern supercomputers, and introducing parallelization can significantly reduce computational cost and accelerates computational efficiency. For further acceleration in the time direction, at least for strongly dissipative problem,the parallel algorithm in \cite{2001R} and many other variants (e.g., the MGRiT algorithm \cite{2015Parallel} and the PFASST algorithm\cite{2012Toward}) perform very well in this regard. However, for wave propagation problems, since the convergence speed largely depends on dissipativity, the performance of these representative algorithms is not satisfactory. In addition, many studies have improved the convergence behavior of the iterative Parallel-in-Time algorithm by modifying the coarse grid correction \cite{2014On,2013Stable,2010Time,2020A,2012Explicit}. As pointed out in \cite{2018Wave}, these modified algorithms either require a large amount of additional computational burden or have very limited applicability.
In this paper, we focus on the PinT algorithm based on diagonalization techniques, which was first proposed by Maday and Rønquist \cite{2008Parallelization}. For linear ordinary differential  systems with initial value, the idea can be described as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime} \left( t \right) +Au\left( t \right) =g\left( t \right) ,
\end{equation*}
where $u\left( 0 \right) =u_0\in \mathbb{R} ^{m\times1}$ is the initial value, $A\in \mathbb{R} ^{m\times m}$ and $g$ are known terms. We discretize the time derivative using a finite difference method and formulate it as an all-at-once fully discrete linear system  
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{M} \boldsymbol{u}:=\left( B\otimes I_x+I_t\otimes A \right) \boldsymbol{u}=\boldsymbol{b},
\label{eq1.1:energy}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{u}=\left[ {u_1}^T,{u_2}^T,\cdots ,{u_n}^T \right] ^T$ with $u_j\approx u\left( t_j \right) $, $\boldsymbol{b}$ contains the initial value and
right-hand-side information, $I_x$ and $I_t$  are tow identity matrices corresponding to space and time,respectively, $B\in \mathbb{R} ^{n\times n}$ is the time discretization matrix. If $B$ is diagonalizable, that is, $B=VDV^{-1}$ with $D=\mathrm{diag}\left( \lambda _{1,}\cdots ,\lambda _n \right) $, we can factorize $\mathbb{M}$ as 
\begin{equation*}
	\mathbb{M} =\left( V\otimes I_x \right) \left( D\otimes I_x+I_t\otimes A \right) \left( V^{-1}\otimes I_x \right).
\end{equation*}
Thus, \eqref{eq1.1:energy} can be directly solved through the following three steps:
\begin{equation}
	\begin{cases}  
		\boldsymbol{g} = (V^{-1} \otimes I_x) \boldsymbol{b},   & \text{step-(a)}; \\  
		(\lambda_j I_x + A) w_j = g_j, j = 1, 2, \ldots, n, &\text{step-(b)};\\  
		\boldsymbol{u} = (V \otimes I_x) \boldsymbol{w}, & \text{step-(c)};  
	\end{cases}
	\label{eq1.2:energy}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{w}=\left( {w_1}^T,{w_2}^T,\cdots ,{w_n}^T \right) ^T$ and $\boldsymbol{g}=\left( {g_1}^T,{g_2}^T,\cdots ,{g_n}^T \right) ^T$. Obviously, in \eqref{eq1.2:energy}, the first and third steps are parallelizable, only need to perform matrix-vector multiplication. The main computational cost comes from the second step. Since there linear systems are completely decoupled, they can be solved in parallel using either direct or iterative solvers.

The key lies in how to accurately and efficiently diagonalize $B$. Using the conventional backward Euler discretization with uniform time steps obviously results in a non-diagonalizable matrix. To achieve diagonalization, different time steps $\left\{ \varDelta t_j \right\} _{j=1}^{n}$ were used in  \cite{2008Parallelization}, which leads to 
\begin{equation*}
B= \begin{bmatrix}
	\frac{1}{\varDelta t_1}&		&		&		\\
	-\frac{1}{\varDelta t_2}&		\frac{1}{\varDelta t_2}&		&		\\
	&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\\
	&		&		-\frac{1}{\varDelta t_n}&		\frac{1}{\varDelta t_n}\\
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation*}
Clearly, the matrix $B$ is diagonalizable. However, in practical applications, the condition number of its eigenvector matrix  $V$  may be very large, which can increase the rounding errors in steps (a) and (b) of \eqref{eq1.2:energy}, thus seriously affecting the accuracy of the numerical solutions. This issue was thoroughly examined by Gander et al. in \cite{2019A}. 
%\begin{equation*}
%	\mathrm{roundoff\ error}=\mathcal{O} \left( \epsilon Cond_2\left( V \right) \right) ,
%\end{equation*}
%where $\epsilon$ is the machine precision. 
 The authors considered geometrically increasing step sizes $\left\{ \varDelta t_j=\varDelta t_1\tau ^{n-1} \right\} _{j=1}^{n}$, and through this choice matrix $B$ can be diagonalized, where $\tau >1$ is a parameter. However, the selection of $\tau$ significantly impacts the error, making it particularly challenging to choose an appropriate $\tau$. To balance rounding errors and discretization errors, numerical results demonstrate that the value of $n$ can only be maintained within a narrow range of approximately 20 to 25. Therefore, parallelism is limited for larger  $n$ . In \cite{2022A}, the authors studied first-order and special second-order partial differential equations. This paper eliminates the undesirable restriction on  $n$  in \cite{2019A}, and generalizes the special second-order form in \cite{2022A} to the general form of first- to third-order partial differential equations. For time discretization, The first $n-1$ time steps employ central finite differences, while the final step utilizes the BDF2 scheme, that is 
\begin{equation}
	 \begin{cases}
		\frac{u_{j+1}-u_{j-1}}{2\varDelta t}+Au_j=g_j, j=1,2,\cdots ,n-1,\\
		\frac{\frac{3}{2}u_n-2u_{n-1}+\frac{1}{2}u_{n-2}}{\varDelta t}+Au_n=g_n.          \\
	\end{cases} 
	\label{eq1.3:energy}
\end{equation}
This implicit time discretization method should not be implemented as a time-stepping scheme due to severe stability issues. For \eqref{eq1.3:energy}, the all-at-once system in the form of \eqref{eq1.1:energy} is specified by
\begin{equation}
	B=\frac{1}{\varDelta t} \begin{bmatrix}
		0&		\frac{1}{2}&		&		&		\\
		-\frac{1}{2}&		0&		\frac{1}{2}&		&		\\
		&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\\
		&		&		-\frac{1}{2}&		0&		\frac{1}{2}\\
		&		&		\frac{1}{2}&		-2&		\frac{3}{2}\\
	\end{bmatrix}  , \boldsymbol{b}=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
		\frac{u_0}{2\varDelta t}+g_1\\
		g_2\\
		\vdots\\
		g_n\\
	\end{array} \right] , \boldsymbol{u}=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
		u_1\\
		u_2\\
		\vdots\\
		u_n\\
	\end{array} \right] .
	\\
	\label{eq1.4:energy}
\end{equation}
 We mention that there are other diagonalization-based PinT algorithms, which use novel preprocessing techniques in \cite{2022A} to handle the all-at-once system \eqref{eq1.1:energy} and perform well for larger  $n$; see, \cite{2022A,2021A,2022Space,2019note,2018A,2020A,2018Preconditioning,2021Matrix}. The hybrid time discretization \eqref{eq1.3:energy} is a famous example of the so-called boundary value methods (BVMs) \cite{1998Solving}, which are widely used in scientific and engineering computing.

Inspired by the pioneering work of \cite{2008Parallelization} and \cite{2022A}, this paper attempts to directly solve the all-at-once system \eqref{eq1.1:energy} (rather than iteratively solving it like \cite{1993Parallel}). By diagonalizing  $B$ \eqref{eq1.4:energy}  into  $B=VDV^{-1}$ , and this demonstrates that through the connection between the characteristic equation and Chebyshev polynomials, the condition number of matrix $V$ satisfies $Cond_2\left( V \right) = \mathcal{O} \left( n^3 \right)$. The spectral decomposition algorithm used in this paper is much faster than the benchmarking algorithm implemented by MATLAB's  $eig$  function.

For the second and third-order nonlinear problems 
\begin{equation*}
	\boldsymbol{u}''(x) \left( t \right) +a_1\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime} \left( t \right) +f\left( u\left( t \right) \right) =0, u\left( 0 \right) =u_0, u^{\prime} \left( 0 \right) =\tilde{u}_0,
\end{equation*}
and
%\begin{equation*}
%	\left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{u}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\boldsymbol{b},
%\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
	\boldsymbol{u}''' +a_1\boldsymbol{u}'' +a_2\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime} +F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =0, u\left( 0 \right) =u_0, u^{\prime} \left( 0 \right) =\tilde{u}_0, u'' \left( 0 \right) =\bar{u}_0,
\end{equation*} 
we prove that after the time discretization \eqref{eq1.3:energy}, the all-at-once system of there tow problems can be written as following:
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathcal{A} \boldsymbol{u}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\boldsymbol{b},
	\end{equation*}
	and
	\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{A}+a_2I \right) \boldsymbol{u}+\mathcal{A}\left( B^{-1}\otimes I_x \right) F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\left( \mathcal{A}+a_2I \right) \boldsymbol{b}_1+\mathcal{A}\left( B^{-1}\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{b}_2,
\end{equation*}
where $\boldsymbol{b}=\left( \frac{a_1u_{0}^{T}}{2\varDelta t}+\frac{{\tilde{u}_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{u_{0}^{T}}{4\varDelta t^2},0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$, $\boldsymbol{b}_1=\left( \frac{a_1{u_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{{u_0}^T}{4\varDelta t^2}, 0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$, $\boldsymbol{b}_2=\left( \frac{a_1{\tilde{u}_0}^T+{\bar{u}_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{{\tilde{u}_0}^T}{4\varDelta t^2},0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$, and $	\mathcal{A}=B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x$, $I=I_t\otimes I_x$, $a_1$ and $a_2$ correspond to the coefficients of the first and second derivatives, respectively. In addition, we also need to mention some relevant fast time-domain algorithms combined with this algorithm, such as the space-time discretization method \cite{2014Space,2015Multilevel}, the low-rank approximation technique \cite{2011Low,2015Matlab}, the domain decomposition algorithm \cite{2015Domain}, and the inversion algorithm \cite{2022A}.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, the direct PinT algorithm for nonlinear problems is introduced.
Section 3 provides specific expressions for matrices  $V$  and  $D$ , and proves the condition number of matrix  $V$  and the diagonalizability of matrix  $B$ \eqref{eq1.4:energy} .
Section 4 present a fast algorithm with complexity  $\mathcal{O} \left( n^2 \right) $  for fast computation of $V^{-1}$ .
Section 5 shows some numerical experiments.
Section 6 concludes this paper.
\section{The PinT algorithm for nonlinear problems} 
\label{sec2}
%\vspace{-3mm}
In this section, We introduce the time discretization and the use of the PinT algorithm based on diagonalization to solve nonlinear problems. We will consider the differential equations with general time derivatives of the first to the third order respectively.

\subsection{First-order problems}\label{sec2.1}
We first consider the following first-order problem
\begin{equation}
u' \left( t \right) +f\left( u\left( t \right) \right) =0, u\left( 0 \right) =u_0,
\label{eq2.1:energy}
\end{equation}
where $t\in \left( 0,T \right) $, $u\left( t \right) \in \mathbb{R} ^m$, and $f: \left( 0,T \right) \times \mathbb{R} ^m\rightarrow \mathbb{R} ^m$. This is a problem of ordinary differential equations, but the algorithm introduced below can also be directly applied to semi-discrete time-dependent partial differential equations. 
For \eqref{eq2.1:energy} similar to \eqref{eq1.3:energy} the time discretization scheme is
\begin{equation}
	 \begin{cases}
	\frac{u_{j+1}-u_{j-1}}{2\varDelta t}+f\left( u_i \right) =0, j=1,2,\cdots ,n-1,\\
	\frac{\frac{3}{2}u_n-2u_{n-1}+\frac{1}{2}u_{n-2}}{\varDelta t}+f\left( u_n \right) =0,          \\
	\end{cases} ,
	\label{eq2.2:energy} 
\end{equation}
where the BDF2 scheme is adopted in the last step, the rest uses the central difference scheme. The all-at-once system of \eqref{eq2.2:energy} is 
\begin{equation}
\left( B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{u}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\boldsymbol{b},
\label{eq2.3:energy}
\end{equation}
where $F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\left( f^T\left( u_1 \right) ,f^T\left( u_2 \right) ,\cdots ,f^T\left( u_n \right) \right) ^T$ and $\boldsymbol{b}=\left( \frac{{u_0}^T}{2\varDelta t},0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$.
Using the standard Newton's iteration for \eqref{eq2.3:energy} can obtain 
\begin{equation}
\left( B\otimes I_x+\nabla F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) \right) \boldsymbol{u}^{k+1}=\boldsymbol{b}+\left( \nabla F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) \boldsymbol{u}^k-F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) \right),
\label{eq2.4:energy}
\end{equation}
where $k\geqslant 0$ is the iteration index and $\nabla F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) =blk\mathrm{diag}\left( \nabla f\left( {u_1}^k \right) ,\cdots ,\nabla f\left( {u_n}^k \right) \right)$ consists of the Jacobian matrix $\nabla f\left( {u_j}^k \right)$ as the $jth$ block. In order for the diagonalization technique to still be applicable, we must replace or approximate all the blocks $\nabla f\left( {u_j}^k \right)$ with a single matrix $A_k$. Drawing on the ideas in \cite{2017Time}, we consider the following average Jacobian matrix 
\begin{equation*}
	A_k:=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^n{\nabla f\left( {u_j}^k \right)}.
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we present a simple Kronecker product to approximate $\nabla F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) $, that is, $\nabla F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) \approx I_t\otimes A_k$.
By substituting this into \eqref{eq2.4:energy}, we arrive at the simplified Newton iteration (SNI):
\begin{equation}
\left( B\otimes I_x+I_t\otimes A_k \right) \boldsymbol{u}^{k+1}=\boldsymbol{b}+\left( \left( I_t\otimes A_k \right) \boldsymbol{u}^k-F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) \right).
\label{eq2.5:energy} 
\end{equation}
SIN is convergent; see, e.g.,\cite{PeterDeuflhard2006Newton} and \cite{1970Iterative}.

Using the same structure, the Jacobian system \eqref{eq2.5:energy} in each SIN can also be solved in parallel in terms of time. If $B$ is diagonalized into $B=VDV^{-1}$, then $\boldsymbol{u}^{k+1}$ in \eqref{eq2.5:energy} can be solved as 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}  
	\boldsymbol{k} = (V^{-1} \otimes I_x) \boldsymbol{r}^k,   & \text{step-(a)}; \\  
	(\lambda_j I_x + A_k) w_j = k_j, j = 1, 2, \ldots, n, &\text{step-(b)};\\  
	\boldsymbol{u}^{k+1} = (V \otimes I_x) \boldsymbol{w}, & \text{step-(c)};  
\end{cases}
\label{eq2.6:energy}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{r}^k=\boldsymbol{b}+\left( \left( I_t\otimes A_k \right) -F\left( \boldsymbol{u}^k \right) \right)$. In the linear case, $f\left( u \right) =Au$, we have $A_k=A$ and $\boldsymbol{r}^k=\boldsymbol{b}$, and therefore, \eqref{eq2.6:energy} reduces to \eqref{eq1.2:energy}. 

\subsection{Second-order problems}\label{sec2.2}
Next, we consider the following second-order differential equation 
\begin{equation}
	u'' \left( t \right) +a_1u' \left( t \right) +f\left( u\left( t \right) \right) =0, u\left( 0 \right) =u_0, u' \left( 0 \right) =\tilde{u}_0, t\in \left( 0,T \right) .
	\label{eq2.7:energy}
\end{equation}
For discretization we let $v\left( t \right) =u' \left( t \right)$ and make an order-reduction by rewriting \eqref{eq2.7:energy} as
\begin{equation}
w\prime \left( t \right) :=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
	u\left( t \right)\\
	v\left( t \right)\\
\end{array} \right] ^{\prime}=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
v\left( t \right)\\
-a_1v-f\left( u\left( t \right) \right)\\
\end{array} \right] =:g\left( w \right) ,  w\left( 0 \right) :=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
u\left( 0 \right)\\
v\left( 0 \right)\\
\end{array} \right] =\left[ \begin{array}{c}
u_0\\
\tilde{u}_0\\
\end{array} \right].
\label{eq2.8:energy}
\end{equation}
In addition, by using the same time discretization scheme as in \eqref{eq2.2:energy}, we have 
\begin{equation}
	 \begin{cases}
		\frac{w_{j+1}-w_{j-1}}{2\varDelta t}+k\left( w_i \right) =0, j=1,2,\cdots ,n-1,\\
		\frac{\frac{3}{2}w_n-2w_{n-1}+\frac{1}{2}w_{n-2}}{\varDelta t}+k\left( w_n \right) =0,\\
	\end{cases}.
	\label{eq2.9:energy}
\end{equation}
Obviously, for \eqref{eq2.9:energy}, the form of the fully simultaneous system is the same as that of \eqref{eq2.3:energy}, so the diagonalization process \eqref{eq2.6:energy} can be directly applied. We introduce the all-at-once system for  $\boldsymbol{u}=\left( {u_1}^T,\cdots ,{u_n}^T \right) ^T$ below, so as to avoid the situation where the storage requirement for the spatial variables at each time point may double.
\begin{lemma}
The vector $\boldsymbol{u}=\left( {u_1}^T,\cdots ,{u_n}^T \right) ^T$ that is discretized in time through \eqref{eq2.9:energy} satisfies 
\begin{equation}
	\left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1 B\otimes I_x  \right) \boldsymbol{u}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\boldsymbol{b},
	\label{eq2.10:energy}
\end{equation}
where B is the matrix defined by \eqref{eq1.4:energy} and $\boldsymbol{b}=\left( \frac{a_1u_{0}^{T}}{2\varDelta t}+\frac{{\tilde{u}_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{u_{0}^{T}}{4\varDelta t^2},0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$.
\begin{proof}
Since $w_j=\left( {u_j}^T,{v_j}^T \right) ^T$, from \eqref{eq2.9:energy} we can represent $\left\{ u_j \right\}$ and $\left\{ v_j \right\}$ separately as 
\begin{align*} 
&\begin{cases}
	\frac{u_{j+1}-u_{j-1}}{2\varDelta t}-v_j=0 ,j=1,2,\cdots ,n-1,\\
	\frac{3u_n-4_{n-1}+u_{n-2}}{2\varDelta t}-v_n=0,\\
\end{cases}\\ 
 &\begin{cases}
	\frac{v_{j+1}-v_{j-1}}{2\varDelta t}+a_1v+f\left( u_j \right) =0, j=1,2,\cdots ,n-1,\\
	\frac{3v_n-4v_{n-1}+v_{n-2}}{2\varDelta t}+a_1v+f\left( u_n \right) =0,\\
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
Therefore, with the matrix $B$ given by \eqref{eq1.4:energy} we have 
\begin{equation}
\left( B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{u}-\boldsymbol{v}=\boldsymbol{b}_1, \left( B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{v}+a_1\boldsymbol{v}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\boldsymbol{b}_2,
\label{eq2.11:energy}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{v}=\left( {v_1}^T,\cdots ,{v_n}^T \right)^T$, $\boldsymbol{b}_1=\left( \frac{{u_0}^T}{2\varDelta t},0,\cdots ,0 \right)^T$ and $\boldsymbol{b}_2=\left( \frac{\tilde{u}_0}{2\varDelta t},0,\cdots , 0 \right)^T $. 
Substituting the left-hand side of Equation \eqref{eq2.11:energy} into the right-hand side gives $\left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{u}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\left( B\otimes I_x+a_1I \right) \boldsymbol{b}_1+\boldsymbol{b}_2$. Through simple calculations, \eqref{eq2.10:energy} can be obtained.
\end{proof} 
\end{lemma}
\subsection{Third-order problems}\label{sec2.3}
Next, we will continue to consider the third-order differential equation  
\begin{equation}
	\boldsymbol{u}''' +a_1\boldsymbol{u}'' +a_2\boldsymbol{u}' +F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =0, u\left( 0 \right) =u_0, u' \left( 0 \right) =\tilde{u}_0, u'' \left( 0 \right) =\bar{u}_0, t\in \left( 0,T \right).
	\label{eq2.12:energy}
\end{equation} 
Similar to the second-order case, let $v\left( t \right) =u' \left( t \right)$ , we can reduce the order of \eqref{eq2.12:energy} to obtain  
\begin{equation}
	w\left( t \right) =\left[ \begin{array}{c}
		u\left( t \right)\\
		v\left( t \right)\\
	\end{array} \right],
	\\
	P=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
		-\bar{u}_0\left( 0 \right) -a_1\tilde{u}_0\left( 0 \right)\\
		a_2v\left( t \right) +F\left( u \right)\\
	\end{array} \right],
	w_0\left( 0 \right) =\left[ \begin{array}{c}
		u_0\\
		\tilde{u}_0\\
	\end{array} \right] =, \tilde{w}_0\left( 0 \right) =\left[ \begin{array}{c}
		\tilde{u}_0\\
		\bar{u}_0\\
	\end{array} \right].
	\label{eq2.13:energy}
\end{equation}
\begin{lemma}
	By using the same discretization as the second-order form, the vector $\boldsymbol{u}=\left( {u_1}^T,\cdots ,{u_n}^T \right)^T$ satisfies 
	\begin{equation}
		\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{A}+a_2I \right) \boldsymbol{u}+\mathcal{A}\left( B^{-1}\otimes I_x \right) F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\left( \mathcal{A}+a_2I \right) \boldsymbol{b}_1+\mathcal{A}\left( B^{-1}\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{b}_2,
		\label{eq2.14:energy}
	\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{b}_1=\left( \frac{a_1{u_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{{u_0}^T}{4\varDelta t^2}, 0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$, $\boldsymbol{b}_2=\left( \frac{a_1{\tilde{u}_0}^T+{\bar{u}_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{{\tilde{u}_0}^T}{4\varDelta t^2},0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$, and $	\mathcal{A}=B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Thus, from \eqref{eq2.13:energy} and \eqref{eq2.12:energy}, we can obtain
\begin{equation}
	\boldsymbol{w}'' +a_1\boldsymbol{w}' +\boldsymbol{P}=0.
	\label{eq2.15:energy}
\end{equation}
There is a second-order form, and \eqref{eq2.15:energy} satisfies 
\begin{equation}
	\left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{w}+\boldsymbol{P}=\boldsymbol{b},
	\label{eq2.16:energy}
\end{equation}
By matrix vectorization, we can obtain 
\begin{equation}
\left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{w}=vec\left( \hat{\boldsymbol{w}}\left( B^2+a_1B \right) ^T \right),
\label{eq2.17:energy}
\end{equation}
where  $\hat{\boldsymbol{w}}=\left( w_1\,\,w_2\,\,\cdots \,\,w_n \right) =\left( \begin{matrix}
	u_1&		u_2&		\cdots&		u_n\\
	v_1&		v_2&		\cdots&		v_n\\
\end{matrix} \right) =\left( \begin{array}{c}
	\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}\\
	\hat{\boldsymbol{v}}\\
\end{array} \right)  $, $vec\left( \hat{\boldsymbol{u}} \right) =\boldsymbol{u}$, $vec\left( \hat{\boldsymbol{v}} \right) =\boldsymbol{v}$, $\left( B^2+a_1B \right) ^T=\left( d_1\,\,d_2\,\,\cdots \,\,d_n \right)$. Substituting \eqref{eq2.17:energy} into \eqref{eq2.16:energy} gives 
\begin{align*}
 &\begin{cases}
	\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}d_1-v\prime _1-a_1v_1=\frac{a_1u_0+\tilde{u}_0}{2\varDelta t}\\
	\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}d_2-v\prime _2-a_1v_2=-\frac{u_0}{4\varDelta t^2}\\
	\vdots\\
	\hat{\boldsymbol{u}}d_n-v\prime _n-a_1v_n=0\\
\end{cases}, 
\\
&\begin{cases}
	\hat{\boldsymbol{v}}d_1+a_2v_1+f_1=\frac{a_1\tilde{u}_0+\bar{u}_0}{2\varDelta t}\\
	\hat{\boldsymbol{v}}d_2+a_2v_2+f_2=-\frac{\tilde{u}_0}{4\varDelta t^2}\\
	\vdots\\
	\hat{\boldsymbol{v}}d_n+a_2v_n+f_n=0\\
\end{cases}.
\end{align*}
Thus, with the matrix $B$ given by \eqref{eq1.4:energy} we have 
\begin{equation}
\left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{u}-\boldsymbol{v}\prime -a_1\boldsymbol{v}=\boldsymbol{b}_1, \left( B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{v}+a_2\boldsymbol{v}+F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\boldsymbol{b}_2,
\label{eq2.18:energy}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{v}=\left( {v_1}^T,\cdots ,{v_n}^T \right) ^T, \boldsymbol{b}_1=\left( \frac{a_1{u_0}^T+{\tilde{u}_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{{u_0}^T}{4\varDelta t^2}, 0\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$, $\boldsymbol{b}_2=\left( \frac{a_1{\tilde{u}_0}^T+{\bar{u}_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}, -\frac{{\tilde{u}_0}^T}{4\varDelta t^2}, 0,\cdots ,0 \right) ^T$. Note that the dimension of the spatial identity matrix here should vary with the spatial dimensions of $\boldsymbol{u}$ and $\boldsymbol{w}$, that is, the $I_x$ corresponding to $\boldsymbol{u}$ is of $m$ dimensions, and the $I_x$ corresponding to $\boldsymbol{w}$ is of $2m$ dimensions. Substituting  $\boldsymbol{v}\prime =\left( B\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{v}-\left( \begin{array}{c}
	\frac{{v_0}^T}{2\varDelta t}\\
	0\\
	\vdots\\
	0\\
\end{array} \right)$  into \eqref{eq2.18:energy} and eliminating $ \boldsymbol{v}$ yields  
\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{A}+a_2I \right) \boldsymbol{u}+\mathcal{A}\left( B^{-1}\otimes I_x \right) F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\left( \mathcal{A}+a_2I \right) \boldsymbol{b}_1+\mathcal{A}\left( B^{-1}\otimes I_x \right) \boldsymbol{b}_2,
\end{equation*}
 where $\mathcal{A}=B^2\otimes I_x+a_1B\otimes I_x$.
\end{proof}
If $f\left( u \right) =Au$, we have $F\left( \boldsymbol{u} \right) =\left( f^T\left( u_1 \right) ,\cdots ,f^T\left( u_n \right) \right) ^T=\left( I_t\otimes A \right) \boldsymbol{u}$, and Substituting it into  the all-at-once-system \eqref{eq2.10:energy} and \eqref{eq2.14:energy} , and since  $B$  is diagonalizable, it is obvious that based on this connection, the previous time-parallel algorithm \eqref{eq2.6:energy} also applies to \eqref{eq2.11:energy} and \eqref{eq2.18:energy}.
%\section{Discussion of \texorpdfstring{{\boldmath$Z=X \cup Y$}}{Z = X union Y}}
\section{The spectral decomposition of $B$}
\label{sec3}
In this section, we will prove that the time-discrete matrix \( B \) is diagonalizable and provide the explicit form of its eigenvector matrix \( V \). Based on these formulas, we estimate the condition number of $V$ in the 2-norm, denoted as $cond\left( V \right) =\mathcal{O} \left( n^3 \right) $. In addition, in the proof of the theorem, we used the explicit formula for the inverse elements of the inverse matrix of the Toeplitz tridiagonal matrix in Reference \cite{1996Matrix,2004On} and the recurrence formula described in Reference \cite{2009Introductory}.

To simplify the notation, we consider rescaling the diagonalization of matrix $B=\frac{1}{\varDelta t}\mathcal{B}$. Clearly, by diagonalizing $\mathcal{B} =V\varSigma{}V^{-1}$, we can obtain 
\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{1}{\Delta t}\mathcal{B}=V(\frac{1}{\Delta t}\varSigma_{})V^{-1}=VDV^{-1}.
\end{equation*}
 Define tow function 
 \begin{equation*}
 T_n\left( x \right) =\cos \left( n\mathrm{arc}\cos x \right) , U_n\left( x \right) =\sin \left[ \left( n+1 \right) \mathrm{arc}\cos x \right] /\sin \left( \mathrm{arc}\cos x \right) ,
 \end{equation*}
 which are the Chebyshev polynomials of the first and second kind, respectively. In the following theorem, we characterize the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the time-discrete matrix $B$ using Chebyshev polynomial representations. Throughout this paper, $i$ denotes the imaginary unit .


\begin{theorem}
	The n eigenvalues of $\mathcal{B}$ are $\lambda _j=ix_j$,with ${\left\{ x_j \right\} ^n}_{j=1}$ being the $n$ roots of 
	\begin{equation}
		U_{n-1}\left( x \right) +iU_{n-2}\left( x \right) -iT_n\left( x \right) +T_{n-1}\left( x \right)=0.
		\label{eq3.1:energy}
    \end{equation}
For each $\lambda _j$, the corresponding eigenvector is $P_j=\left[ p_{j,0},\cdots ,p_{j,n-1} \right] ^T$,and 
   \begin{equation}
	p_{j,k}=i^kU_k\left( x_j \right) , k=0,\cdots ,n-1,
	\label{eq3.2:energy}
	\end{equation}
where $p_{j,0}=1$ is assumed for normalization.
\begin{proof}
	Let $\lambda \in \mathbb{C}$ be an eigenvalue of matrix $\mathcal{B}$ , and $P=\left[ p_0,p_1,\cdots ,p_{n-1} \right] ^T\ne 0$ be the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda \in \mathbb{C}$. Thus, from $\mathcal{B} P=\lambda P$ we can obtain 
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{cases}
		\lambda p_0=\frac{p_1}{2},\\
		\lambda p_1=-\frac{p_0}{2}+\frac{p_2}{2},\\
		\vdots\\
		\lambda p_{n-2}=-\frac{p_{n-3}}{2}+\frac{p_{n-1}}{2},\\
		\lambda p_{n-1}=\frac{3}{2}p_{n-1}-2p_{n-2}+\frac{1}{2}p_{n-3}.\\
	\end{cases}
	\label{eq3.3:energy}
	\end{equation}
	Clearly, $p_0\ne 0$, otherwise,$p_1=\cdots =p_{n-1}=0$. Without loss of generality, we normalize $p_0$, that is, assume $p_0=1$ , so that we can obtain $p_1=2\lambda$   and recursively derive 
	\begin{equation}
	2\lambda p_{k-1}=p_k-p_{k-2}.
	\label{eq3.4:energy}
	\end{equation}
	holds for $k=2,\cdots ,n-1$, and the last equation gives
	\begin{equation}
	\left( 2\lambda -3 \right) p_{n-1}=p_{n-3}-4p_{n-2}.
	\label{eq3.5:energy}
	\end{equation}
	Let $\lambda =\frac{1}{2}\left( y-\frac{1}{y} \right) =i\cos \theta$ with $y=ie^{i\theta}$. The general solution form of the recursive \eqref{eq3.4:energy} is
	\begin{equation}
		p_k=c_1y^k+c_2\left( -y \right) ^{-k}.
		\label{eq3.6:energy}
	\end{equation}
	From the initial conditions $p_0=1$  and  $p_1=2\lambda =y-y^{-1}$ , it can be obtained that
	\begin{equation*}
	c_1+c_2=1, c_1y-c_2y^{-1}=y-y^{-1},
	\end{equation*}
	which gives $c_1=\frac{y}{y+y^{-1}}$ and $c_2=\frac{y^{-1}}{y+y^{-1}}$. In addition, by substituting $y=ie^{i\theta}$ , we can obtain  
	\begin{equation}
		p_k=\frac{y^{k+1}+\left( -1 \right) ^ky^{-\left( k+1 \right)}}{y+y^{-1}}=\frac{i^k\sin \left[ \left( k+1 \right) \theta \right]}{\sin \theta}, k=0,\cdots ,n-1.
		\label{eq3.7:energy}
	\end{equation}
	In view of $\lambda =i\cos \theta$, we rewrite \eqref{eq3.5:energy} as 
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{i^{n-3}\sin \left[ \left( n-2 \right) \theta \right]}{\sin \theta}-4\frac{i^{n-2}\sin \left[ \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right]}{\sin \theta}=\left( 2i\cos \theta -3 \right) \frac{i^{n-1}\sin \left( n\theta \right)}{\sin \theta},
	\end{equation*}
	which is equivalent to 
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\sin \left( n\theta \right)}{\sin \theta}+i\frac{\sin \left[ \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right]}{\sin \theta}=i\cos \left( n\theta \right) -\cos \left[ \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right] .
		\label{eq3.8:energy}
	\end{equation}
	This is a polynomial equation in $\lambda =i\cos \theta $ and has a dimension of $n$ due to $\frac{\sin \left( n\theta \right)}{\sin \theta}=U_{n-1}\left( -i\lambda \right)$, $\frac{\sin \left[ \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right]}{\sin \theta}=U_{n-2}\left( -i\lambda \right)$, $\cos \left( n\theta \right) =T_n\left( -i\lambda \right)$, $\cos \left[ \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right] =T_{n-1}\left( -i\lambda \right)$ are polynomials of $\lambda$ with degrees $n-1$ and $n-2$ and $n$ and $n-1$, respectively.
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}
Denote $\lambda =ix$ with $x=\cos \theta $. From \eqref{eq3.7:energy} and \eqref{eq3.8:energy}, we can obtain 
\begin{equation}
	p_k=i^kU_k\left( x \right) ,k=0,1,\cdots ,n-1,
	\label{eq3.9:energy}
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
		U_{n-1}\left( x \right) +iU_{n-2}\left( x \right)+T_{n-1}\left( x \right)-iT_n\left( x \right)=0.
		\label{eq3.10:energy}
	\end{equation}
The $n$ roots $x_1,x_2,\cdots ,x_n$ of \eqref{eq3.10:energy} give the $n$ eigenvalues $\lambda _j=ix_j$ of the matrix $\mathcal{B}$. And \eqref{eq3.9:energy} gives the eigenvectors corresponding to each eigenvalue.

Based on Theorem 3.1, we further prove that the matrix  $\mathcal{B}$  has  $n$  distinct eigenvalues, that is,  $\mathcal{B}$  is diagonalizable, It is equivalent to that $B$  is diagonalizable.
\begin{theorem}
$U_{n-1}\left( x \right) +iU_{n-2}\left( x \right)+T_{n-1}\left( x \right)-iT_n\left( x \right)=0$ has n distinct roots, and if x is a root, then so is $-\bar{x}$.
\begin{proof}
For the convenience of proof, we consider the  matrix $\mathcal{B}$ of order $n+1$ here, we first prove that the $n+1-orde$r matrix  $\mathcal{B}$  has  $n+1$  distinct eigenvalues. From equation \eqref{eq3.1:energy}, it can be seen  that there are no real roots (that is, $\lambda _j$ is not a pure imaginary number). Let the characteristic polynomial of matrix  $\mathcal{B}$  be  
\begin{equation}
f\left( \lambda \right) =\left| \lambda E-\mathcal{B} \right|=\left| \begin{matrix}
	D&		\alpha\\
	\beta&		a\\
\end{matrix} \right|,
\label{eq3.11:energy}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
D=\left( \begin{matrix}
	\lambda&		-\frac{1}{2}&		&		\\
	\frac{1}{2}&		\lambda&		\ddots&		\\
	&		\ddots&		\ddots&		-\frac{1}{2}\\
	&		&		\frac{1}{2}&		\lambda\\
\end{matrix} \right) ,  \alpha =\left( \begin{matrix}
	0&		\cdots&		0&		-\frac{1}{2}\\
\end{matrix} \right) ^T,  \beta =\left( 0,\cdots ,0,-\frac{1}{2},2 \right),  a=\lambda -\frac{3}{2},
\end{equation*} 
and $D\in R^{n\times n}, \alpha \in R^{n\times 1}, \beta \in R^{1\times n}$. Define $$P=\left( \begin{matrix}
	I&		0\\
	-\beta D^{-1}&		1\\
\end{matrix} \right) ,  Q=\left( \begin{matrix}
	I&		-D^{-1}\alpha\\
	0&		1\\
\end{matrix} \right) ,$$
where $I\in R^{n\times n}$. Thus, from $P\left( \begin{matrix}
	D&		\alpha\\
	\beta&		a\\
\end{matrix} \right) Q=\left( \begin{matrix}
	D&		0\\
	0&		a-\beta D^{-1}\alpha\\
\end{matrix} \right)$, it is known that 
\begin{equation}
\left| \begin{matrix}
	D&		\alpha\\
	\beta&		a\\
\end{matrix} \right|=\left| \begin{matrix}
	D&		0\\
	0&		a-\beta D^{-1}\alpha\\
\end{matrix} \right|=\left| D \right|\left| a-\beta D^{-1}\alpha \right|
\\,
\label{eq3.12:energy}
\end{equation}
Since $\lambda_j$ is not a pure imaginary number, when $\lambda=\lambda_j$, $D$ is invertible, that is, \eqref{eq3.12:energy} holds when $\lambda=\lambda_j$. So $f\left( \lambda \right) =0$ is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
 a-\beta D^{-1}\alpha =0.
 \label{eq3.13:energy}
\end{equation}
 Let $D^{-1}=\left( d_{ij} \right) , i,j=1,2,\cdots ,n$, then $\beta D^{-1}\alpha =\frac{1}{4}d_{n-1,n}-d_{nn}$. Since the explicit formula for the elements of the inverse matrix of a Toeplitz tridiagonal matrix is 
 \begin{equation*}
 	d_{ij}=\left( -1 \right) ^{i+j}\left( \frac{c}{b} \right) ^{\frac{j-i}{2}}\frac{U_{i-1}\left( \theta \right) U_{n-j}\left( \theta \right)}{\sqrt{bc}U_n\left( \theta \right)},  i\leqslant j,
 \end{equation*}
 Here, $a$, $b$, and $c$ correspond to the elements of the main diagonal, upper diagonal, and subdiagonal respectively, and $\theta =\frac{a}{2\sqrt{bc}}$, $n$ represents the dimension of $D$.  it follows that 
  \begin{equation}
  	d_{n-1,n}=-2\frac{U_{n-2}\left( \theta \right)}{U_n\left( \theta \right)},  d_{nn}=-2i\frac{U_{n-1}\left( \theta \right)}{U_n\left( \theta \right)},
  	\label{eq3.14:energy}
  \end{equation}
  where $U_n\left( \theta \right)$  is the Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind, and $\theta =\frac{a}{2\sqrt{bc}}=-i\lambda $, substituting \eqref{eq3.14:energy} into \eqref{eq3.13:energy} and the recurrence relation of Chebyshev polynomials, we have
  \begin{align*}
  	&\lambda -\frac{3}{2}=\frac{1}{4U_n\left( \theta \right)}\left( -2U_{n-2}\left( \theta \right) +8iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right) \right), 
  	\\
  	&2U_n\left( \theta \right) \left( \lambda -\frac{3}{2} \right) =-U_{n-2}\left( \theta \right) +4iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right), 
  	\\
  	&2U_n\left( \theta \right) \left( \lambda -\frac{3}{2} \right) =-\left( 2\theta U_{n-1}\left( \theta \right) -U_n\left( \theta \right) \right) +4iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right), 
  	\\
  \end{align*}
simplifying the above equation briefly, we can get
\begin{equation}
\left( 2\lambda -4 \right) U_n\left( \theta \right) =\left( 2\lambda +4 \right) iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right).
\label{eq3.15:energy}
\end{equation}
From the relationship between Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind and the second kind, \eqref{eq3.15:energy} can be transformed into  
\begin{equation}
	\left( 2\lambda -4 \right) T_n\left( \theta \right) =\left( 2\lambda ^2-2\lambda +4 \right) iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right),
	\label{eq3.16:energy}
\end{equation}
taking the derivative of both sides of \eqref{eq3.16:energy} with respect to $\lambda$ yields 
\begin{equation*}
	2T_n\left( \theta \right) -\left( 2\lambda -4 \right) nU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right) i=\left( 4\lambda -2 \right) iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right) +\left( 2\lambda ^2-2\lambda +4 \right) i\frac{nT_n\left( \theta \right) -\theta U_{n-1}\left( \theta \right)}{\theta ^2-1}\left( -i \right).
\end{equation*}
Simplifying the above equation gives  
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\left( \lambda ^2+1 \right) \left[ 4\lambda -2+n\left( 2\lambda -4 \right) \right] iU_{n-1}\left( \theta \right) -2\left( \lambda ^2+1 \right) T_n\left( \theta \right)\\
& -n\left( 2\lambda ^2-2\lambda +4 \right) T_n\left( \theta \right) -i\lambda \left( 2\lambda ^2-2\lambda +4 \right) U_{n-1}\left( \theta \right) =0,
\end{aligned}
\label{eq3.17:energy}
\end{equation}
If \eqref{eq3.16:energy} has a multiple root  $\lambda_j$ , substituting  $\lambda_j$  into \eqref{eq3.17:energy} makes the equation hold. Therefore, we may assume that \eqref{eq3.16:energy} has a multiple root  $\lambda_j$ , and substituting \eqref{eq3.16:energy} into \eqref{eq3.17:energy} yields  
\begin{align*}
\lbrack &\left( \lambda _{j}^{2}+1 \right) \left( 2\lambda _j-4 \right) \left( 4\lambda _j-2+n\left( 2\lambda _j-4 \right) \right) -2\left( \lambda _{j}^{2}+1 \right) \left( 2\lambda _{j}^{2}-2\lambda _j+4 \right) -n\left( 2\lambda _{j}^{2}-2\lambda _j+4 \right) ^2\\
&-\lambda _j\left( 2\lambda _j-4 \right) \left( 2\lambda _{j}^{2}-2\lambda _j+4 \right)  \rbrack U_{n-1}\left( \theta_j \right) =0,
\end{align*}
by simplifying the aforementioned equation, one obtains  
\begin{equation}
\left[ -\left( 4+8n \right) \lambda _{j}^{3}-12\lambda _{j}^{2} \right] U_{n-1}\left( \theta _j \right) =0.
\label{eq3.18:energy}
\end{equation}
 It is obvious that $-\left( 4+8n \right) \lambda _{j}^{3}-12\lambda _{j}^{2}$ has two roots as $0$ and one root as $-\frac{12}{4+8n}$, all of which are real roots. Therefore, when $\lambda =\lambda _j$, $-\left( 4+8n \right) \lambda _{j}^{3}-12\lambda _{j}^{2}$ is not zero.
 Therefore, for \eqref{eq3.18:energy} to hold, it can only be that  $U_n\left( \theta_j \right) =0,$
substituting it into \eqref{eq3.16:energy} is equivalent to  $\left( 2\lambda_j -4 \right) T_n\left( \theta_j \right)=0$, that is $T_n\left( \theta_j \right)=0$, Thus,  $\theta_j $  is a common root of  $T_n\left( \theta \right)$  and  $U_n\left( \theta \right)$. Since  $T_n\left( \theta \right)$  and  $U_n\left( \theta \right)$   are Chebyshev polynomials of the first and second kinds respectively, and there are no common roots between Chebyshev polynomials of the first and second kinds, a contradiction is derived. That is, the assumption does not hold, so \eqref{eq3.16:energy} has no multiple roots. Therefore, \eqref{eq3.11:energy} has  $n+1$  distinct roots, meaning the matrix  $\mathcal{B}$  has  $n+1$  distinct eigenvalues. Thus, it is equivalent to that when $\mathcal{B}$ is of order $n$, there are $n$ distinct eigenvalues, so it is diagonalizable.


	By the properties of the first and second kind Chebyshev polynomials, we can obtain  
	\begin{equation*}
		T_n\left( x \right) =\left( -1 \right) ^nT_n\left( -x \right) , U_{n-1}\left( x \right) =\left( -1 \right) ^{n-1}U_{n-1}\left( -x \right).
	\end{equation*}
	If $x$ is a root, then 
	\begin{align*}
	U_{n-1}\left( -\bar{x} \right) +iU_{n-2}\left( -\bar{x} \right) &=\left( -1 \right) ^{n-1}\bar{U}_{n-1}\left( x \right) +\left( -1 \right) ^{n-1}i\bar{U}_{n-2}\left( x \right)\\ &=\left( -1 \right) ^{n-1}\left( \bar{U}_{n-1}\left( x \right) +i\bar{U}_{n-2}\left( x \right) \right)\\
	& =\left( -1 \right) ^{n-1}\left( -i\bar{T}_n\left( x \right) -\bar{T}_{n-1}\left( x \right) \right)\\
	& =\left( -1 \right) ^ni\bar{T}_n\left( x \right) -\left( -1 \right) ^{n-1}\bar{T}_{n-1}\left( x \right) \\
	&=iT_n\left( -\bar{x} \right) -T_{n-1}\left( -\bar{x} \right) .
	\end{align*}
	Therefore, $-\bar{x}$ is also a root.
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}

According to Theorem 3.2, we conclude that $\mathcal{B}$ is diagonalizable. Let the diagonalization of $\mathcal{B}$ be $\mathcal{B} =V\varSigma{V}$, where $\varSigma{=\mathrm{diag}\left( \lambda _1,\cdots ,\lambda _n \right)}$ and 
\begin{equation}
V=\left[ P_1,P_2,\cdots ,P_n \right] =\underset{:=I}{\underbrace{\mathrm{diag}\left( i^0,i^1,\cdots ,i^{n-1} \right) }}\underset{:=\varPhi}{\underbrace{ \begin{bmatrix}
			U_0\left( x_1 \right)  &  \cdots  &  U_0\left( x_n \right)\\
			\vdots  &  \cdots  &  \vdots\\
			U_{n-1}\left( x_1 \right)  &  \cdots  &  U_{n-1}\left( x_n \right)\\
		\end{bmatrix}}}=I\varPhi,
	\label{eq3.19:energy}
	\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \lambda _j \right\} _{j=1}^{n}$ and $\left\{ x_j \right\} _{j=0}^{n-1}$ are given by theorem 3.1. In \eqref{eq3.19:energy} $I$ is identity matrix and $\varPhi $ is a Vandermonde matrix \cite{2012Differential} defined by Chebyshev orthogonal polynomials. Thus, we can obtain 
\begin{equation}
Cond_2\left( V \right) =Cond_2\left( I\varPhi \right) =Cond_2\left( \varPhi \right).
\label{eq3.20:energy}
\end{equation}
The following theorem will further prove that $Cond_2\left( V \right) =\mathcal{O} \left( n^3 \right) $, which means that as $n$ increases, the rounding errors generated by the diagonalization process only increase moderately.
\begin{lemma}
Let $Q=\left\{ q_{ij} \right\} $ be an $n\times n$ matrix, we have $tr\left( Q \right) =\sum_{i=1}^n{\lambda _i}$ and $\max \left\{ \lambda _1,\lambda _2,\cdots ,\lambda _n \right\} \leqslant \sum_{i=1}^n{\left| q_{ii} \right|}$, where $\lambda_i$ is an eigenvalue of Q and $j,i=1,2,\cdots ,n$.
\begin{proof}
Let the characteristic polynomial of $Q$ be $f=\lambda ^n+a_{n-1}\lambda ^{n-1}+\cdots +a_1\lambda +a_0$. Then, according to Vieta's formulas, we can obtain $a_{n-1}=-\left( \lambda _1+\lambda _2+\cdots +\lambda _n \right)$. Since $tr\left( Q \right) =\sum_{i=1}^n{q_{ii}}$, and the characteristic polynomial of $Q$ is $\left| \lambda I-Q \right|=\begin{vmatrix}
	\lambda -q_{11}&		\cdots&		-q_{1n}\\
	\,\,\vdots&		\cdots&		\,\,\vdots\\
	-q_{n1}&		\cdots&		\lambda -q_{nn}\\
\end{vmatrix}$. By expanding it, we can see that the coefficient of $\lambda ^{n-1}$ is $a_{n-1}=-\sum_{i=1}^n{q_{ii}}=-tr\left( Q \right)$. Thus,  $tr\left( Q \right) =\sum_{i=1}^n{\lambda _i}$.
\end{proof}
\end{lemma}
\begin{theorem}
For the eigenvector matrix $V$, it holds
\begin{equation}
	Cond_2\left( V \right) =\mathcal{O} \left( n^3 \right).
	\label{eq3.21:energy} 
\end{equation}
\begin{proof}
	For each $s=1,\cdots ,n,$ we denote $\theta _s=\frac{s\pi}{n+1}$ and $y_s=\cos \theta _s$ Let
\begin{equation}
	L_j\left( x \right) =\prod_{1\leqslant k\leqslant n,k\ne j}^{}{\frac{x-x_k}{x_j-x_k}}=\frac{U_{n-1}\left( x \right) +iU_{n-2}\left( x \right) -iT_n\left( x \right) +T_{n-1}\left( x \right)}{\left( x-x_j \right) \left[ {U^{\prime}}_{n-1}\left( x \right) +{iU^{\prime}}_{n-2}\left( x \right) -{iT^{\prime}}_n\left( x \right) +{T^{\prime}}_{n-1}\left( x \right) \right]},
	\label{eq3.22:energy}
\end{equation}
the Lagrange interpolation polynomial $L_j\left( x_k \right) =\delta _{jk}$ and both $x_k$, $x_j$ are roots of \eqref{eq3.1:energy} , where $\delta _{jk}$ is the standard Kronecker delta and $j=1,\cdots ,n.$ Let's estimate $\left\| \theta \right\| _2$ first, using the Christoffel-Darboux formula, we can obtain  
\begin{equation}
\left( \varPhi ^*\varPhi \right) _{jk}=\sum_{l=0}^{n-1}{U_l\left( \bar{x}_j \right)}U_l\left( x_k \right) =\frac{U_n\left( \bar{x}_j \right) U_{n-1}\left( x_k \right) -U_{n-1}\left( \bar{x}_j \right) U_n\left( x_k \right)}{2\left( \bar{x}_j-x_k \right)}.
\label{eq3.23:energy}
\end{equation}
 Let $\theta _j=\alpha _j+i\beta _j$, so $x_j=\cos \theta _j$, from \eqref{eq3.23:energy} for any $k=1,2,\cdots ,n$ we get
 \begin{align*}
  \left| \left( \varPhi ^*\varPhi \right) _{kk} \right|&=\left| \sum_{l=0}^{n-1}{U_l\left( \bar{x}_k \right)}U_l\left( x_k \right) \right|=\left| \frac{U_n\left( \bar{x}_k \right) U_{n-1}\left( x_k \right) -U_{n-1}\left( \bar{x}_k \right) U_n\left( x_k \right)}{2\left( \bar{x}_k-x_k \right)} \right|\\
  &=\left| \frac{\sin \left[ \left( n+1 \right) \bar{\theta}_k \right] \sin \left( n\theta _k \right) -\sin \left[ \left( n+1 \right) \theta _k \right] \sin \left( n\bar{\theta}_k \right)}{2\sin \bar{\theta}\sin \theta \left( \bar{x}_k-x_k \right)} \right|\\
  &\leqslant \frac{1}{2\left| \sin \alpha \sin \beta \right|\left( \sin ^2\alpha \cosh ^2\beta +\cos ^2\alpha \sinh ^2\beta \right)}\leqslant \frac{1}{2\left| \sin \alpha \right|\left| \sinh \beta \right|^3}\\
  &\leqslant \frac{1}{2\left| \alpha -\frac{\alpha ^3}{6} \right|\left| \beta \right|^3}.  
  \end{align*}
From the initial value of the iteration in section 4.1, we have $\alpha =\mathcal{O} \left( n^{-1} \right)$ and $\beta =\mathcal{O} \left( n^{-1} \right)$, then $\frac{1}{2\left| \alpha -\frac{\alpha ^3}{6} \right|\left| \beta \right|^3}= \mathcal{O} \left( n^{4} \right)$, $\sum_{k=1}^n{\left| \left( \varPhi ^*\varPhi \right) _{kk} \right|}\leqslant \mathcal{O} \left( n^5 \right)$, 
 \begin{equation}
 	\left\| \varPhi \right\| _2=\sqrt{\rho \left( \varPhi ^*\varPhi \right)}\leqslant \sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^n{\left| \varPhi ^*\varPhi \right|_{kk}}}\leqslant \mathcal{O} \left( n^{\frac{5}{2}} \right).
 	\label{eq3.24:energy}
 \end{equation}
 Let $W={\left( w_{jk} \right) ^n}_{j,k=1}=\varPhi ^{-1}$, we obtain from orthogonality and Gaussian quadeature formula that 
 \begin{equation}
 	w_{jk}=\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{-1}^1{L_j\left( x \right) U_{k-1}\left( x \right) \sqrt{1-x^2}dx}=\sum_{s=1}^n{\frac{2\left( 1-{y_s}^2 \right)}{n+1}L_j}\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right).
 	\label{eq3.25:energy}
 \end{equation}
 A simple calculation yields 
 \begin{equation*}
 	\left( WW^* \right) _{jk}=\sum_{s=1}^n{\frac{2\left( 1-{y_s}^2 \right)}{n+1}}L_j\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_k\left( y_s \right),
 \end{equation*}
 we denote $\left| L_p\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_s \right) \right|=\max \left\{ \left| L_1\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_1\left( y_s \right) \right|, \cdots , \left| L_n\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_n\left( y_s \right) \right| \right\}$,\\ $\left| L_p\left( y_q \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_q \right) \right|=\max \left\{ \left| L_p\left( y_1 \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_1 \right) \right|, \cdots , \left| L_p\left( y_n \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_n \right) \right| \right\} $, therefore, we can conclude that 
 \begin{align*}
 \sum_{k=1}^n{\left| \left( WW^* \right) _{kk} \right|}&=\left| \sum_{s=1}^n{\frac{2\left( 1-{y_s}^2 \right)}{n+1}}L_k\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_k\left( y_s \right) \right|=\frac{2}{n+1}\left| \sum_{s=1}^n{\left( 1-y_s \right) \sum_{k=1}^n{L_k\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_k\left( y_s \right)}} \right|\\
 &\leqslant \frac{2n}{n+1} \sum_{s=1}^n{\left( 1-y_s \right) \left| L_p\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_s \right) \right|} \leqslant \frac{2n}{n+1}\sum_{s=1}^n{\left| L_p\left( y_s \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_s \right) \right|}\\
 &\leqslant \frac{2n^2}{n+1}\left| L_p\left( y_q \right) \bar{L}_p\left( y_q \right) \right|=\mathcal{O} \left( n \right),
 \end{align*}
 and
 \begin{equation}
 \left\| W \right\| _2=\sqrt{\rho \left( WW^* \right)}\leqslant \sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^n{\left| \left( WW^* \right) _{kk} \right|}}=\mathcal{O} \left( n^{\frac{1}{2}} \right). 
 \label{eq3.26:energy}
 \end{equation}
 Combining \eqref{eq3.24:energy} and \eqref{eq3.26:energy} gives $Cond_2\left( V \right) =Cond_2\left( \varPhi \right) =\left\| \varPhi \right\| _2\left\| \varPhi ^{-1} \right\| _2=\left\| \varPhi \right\| _2\left\| W \right\| _2=\mathcal{O} \left( n^3 \right).$
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}

\section{Fast implementation}
\label{sec4}
In this section, It can be observed that the acceleration of Algorithm \eqref{eq2.6:energy} can be achieved by fast computation of the spectral decomposition of $B$ and the inversion of $V^{-1}$. we present a fast algorithm for  $V^{-1}$  and a fast spectral decomposition for  $B$ .

\subsection{Fast spectral decomposition of $B$}
The spectral decomposition of the time-discrete matrix  $B=VDV^{-1}$  is crucial in our PinT algorithm. The eigenvalues  $\lambda_j$  can be calculated via Newton's iterative method, as detailed below. According to Theorem 3.1 (Equation 3.8), we have  $\lambda _j=i\cos \left( \theta _j \right)$, where $\theta _j$ is the $jth$ root of 
\begin{equation*}
	\rho \left( \theta \right) :=\sin \left( n\theta \right) +i\sin \left( \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right) +\sin \left( \theta \right) \cos \left( \left( n-1 \right) \theta \right) -i\sin \left( \theta \right) \cos \left( n\theta \right) =0.
\end{equation*}
Applying Newton’s iteration to the nonlinear equation $\rho \left( \theta \right)$ of single variable $\theta$ leads to
\begin{equation}
\theta _{j}^{\left( l+1 \right)}=\theta _{j}^{\left( l \right)}-\frac{\rho \left( \theta _{j}^{\left( l \right)} \right)}{\rho \prime \left( \theta _{j}^{\left( l \right)} \right)}, l=0,1,2,\cdots .
\label{eq4.1:energy}
\end{equation}
Such Newton's iterative method requires  $n$  iterations for  $n$  eigenvalues  $\lambda_j$ . The number of iterations for all  $n$  eigenvalues remains almost constant, so the complexity of calculating all eigenvalues via the Newton iteration in \eqref{eq4.1:energy} is  $\mathcal{O} \left( n \right) $ . However, selecting  $n$  initial guesses  $\left\{ \theta _{j}^{\left( l \right)} \right\} _{j=1}^{n}$  is very difficult, as  $n$  iterations must converge to  $n$  distinct values—meaning not all eigenvalues can be found. Here, we choose the initial guesses as  
\begin{equation*}
	\theta _{j}^{\left( 0 \right)}=k\pi \left( \frac{c}{n}+\frac{1-c}{n+1} \right) +i\left( \frac{1-c}{n-2}+\frac{c}{n-1} \right) , c=0.7, j=1,2,\cdots ,n,
\end{equation*}
by which the iterates of \eqref{eq4.1:energy} converge to the $n$ different eigenvalues correctly. In Section 5.1, we presented the results of this experiment.
\subsection{ A fast $\mathcal{O} \left( n^2 \right) $ algorithm for computing $V^{-1}$}
According to \eqref{eq3.19:energy}, the eigenvector matrix  $V$  of matrix  $\mathcal{B}$  satisfies  $V=I\theta \,\,$ with $I=\mathrm{diag}\left( i^0,i^1,\cdots ,i^{n-1} \right)$ . To solve for  $V^{-1}=\theta ^{-1}I^{-1}$  is essentially to determine  $W=\theta ^{-1}$ . In this part, we propose a fast method for solving  $V^{-1}$ .

Simply apply the recurrence relation of the Chebyshev polynomials of the second kind $2yU_j\left( y \right) =U_{j+1}\left( y \right) +U_{j-1}\left( y \right)$ gives 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
4{y_s}^2U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) &=2y_s\left[ U_{k-2}\left( y_s \right) +U_k\left( y_s \right) \right]\\
   &= \begin{cases}
	U_{k-3}\left( y_s \right) +2U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) +U_{k+1}\left( y_s \right) , 2\leqslant k\leqslant n-1,\\
	U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) +U_{k+1}\left( y_s \right) ,   k=1,\\
	U_{k-3}\left( y_s \right) +U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) ,  k=n.\\
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq4.2:energy}
\end{equation}
It then follows from \eqref{eq3.25:energy} that
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
2w_{jk}&=\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{s=1}^n 4L_j\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) -\frac{1}{n+1} \sum_{s=1}^n 4y_{s}^{2}L_j\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) \\
		&= \begin{cases}
			2\psi _{j,k}-\psi _{j,k-2}-\psi _{j,k+2}, 2\leqslant k\leqslant n-1,\\
			3\psi _{j,k}-\psi _{j,k-2},   k=1,\\
			3\psi _{j,k}-\psi _{j,k+2},   k=n,\\
		\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq4.3:energy}
\end{equation}
where $\psi _{j,k}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{s=1}^n{L_j\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right)}$. Since $U_n\left( y_s \right) =0$, we have $\psi _{j,n+1}=0$ for $j=1,2,\cdots ,n.$ Define 
\begin{equation}
	p_n\left( x \right) =U_{n-1}\left( x \right) +T_{n-1}\left( x \right) +iU_{n-2}\left( x \right) -iT_n\left( x \right) , b_k=\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{s=1}^n{p_n\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right)}.
	\label{eq4.4:energy}
\end{equation}
from \eqref{eq3.22:energy} that $p_n\left( x \right) =p_{n}^{\prime}\left( x \right) \left( y_s-x_j \right) L_j\left( y_s \right).$ Thus, 


\begin{equation}
\frac{2b_k}{p_{n}^{\prime}\left( x_j \right)}=\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{s=1}^n{2\left( y_s-x_j \right) L_j\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right) =\psi _{j,k-1}+\psi _{j,k+1}-2x_j\psi _{j,k}}.
\label{eq4.5:energy}
\end{equation}
To evaluate $b_k$, we investigate the integral of $p_n\left( x \right) U_{k-1}\left( x \right) \sqrt{1-x^2}$ on [-1,1]. According to \eqref{eq4.2:energy} and \eqref{eq4.4:energy}, we can obtain by the Gaussian quadrature formula that 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\frac{4}{\pi}\int_{-1}^1 p_n\left( x \right) U_{k-1}\left( x \right) \sqrt{1-x^2}dx
			&=\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{s=1}^n 4\left( 1-y_{s}^{2} \right) p_n\left( y_s \right) U_{k-1}\left( y_s \right)\\
				 &= \begin{cases}
					2b_k-b_{k-2}-b_{k+2}, 2\leqslant k\leqslant n-1,\\
					3b_k-b_{k+2},  k=1,\\
					3b_k-b_{k-2}, k=n.\\
				\end{cases}
	\end{aligned}
	\label{eq4.6:energy}
\end{equation}


Additionally, it can be calculated according to the orthogonality of Chebyshev polynomials that 
\begin{equation}
\frac{4}{\pi}\int_{-1}^1{p_n\left( x \right) U_{k-1}\left( x \right) \sqrt{1-x^2}dx}=2\delta _{n-1,k-1}+\delta _{n-1,k-1}-\delta _{n-3,k-1}+3i\delta _{n-2,k-1}-i\delta _{n,k-1}.
\label{eq4.7:energy}
\end{equation}
By combining Eqs. \eqref{eq4.6:energy} and \eqref{eq4.7:energy} above, a linear system can be obtained that 
\begin{equation}
	S_n\boldsymbol{b}:=\begin{pmatrix}
		3&		0&		-1&		&		&		&		\\
		0&		2&		0&		-1&		&		&		\\
		-1&		0&		2&		0&		-1&		&		\\
		&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\ddots&		\\
		&		&		-1&		0&		2&		0&		-1\\
		&		&		&		-1&		0&		2&		0\\
		&		&		&		&		-1&		0&		3\\
	\end{pmatrix} \left( \begin{array}{c}
		b_1\\
		b_2\\
		b_3\\
		\vdots\\
		b_{n-2}\\
		b_{n-1}\\
		b_n\\
	\end{array} \right) =\left( \begin{array}{c}
		0\\
		0\\
		0\\
		\vdots\\
		-1\\
		3i\\
		3\\
	\end{array} \right).
	\label{eq4.8:energy}
\end{equation}
Let $\varPsi =\left[ \psi _{jk} \right] .$ A fast algorithm for finding the inverse of  $W=\varPhi ^{-1}$  is given by the following three steps.


Step-1: First, solve for  $\boldsymbol{b}=\left[ b_1,b_2,\cdots ,b_n \right] ^T$  in \eqref{eq4.8:energy}, and its computational complexity is  $\mathcal{O} \left( n \right) $ .


Step-2: Based on the fact $\psi _{j,n+1}=0$, the $jth$ row $\boldsymbol{\varPsi }_j=\left[ \psi _{j,1},\varPsi _{j,2},\cdots ,\psi _{j,n} \right] $ of $\varPsi$ can be solved from a sequence of sparse tridiagonal linear systems (for each $j=1,2,\cdots ,n$)
\begin{equation}
G_j\psi _{j}^{T}:=Tri\mathrm{diag}\left\{ 1,-2x_j,1 \right\} \boldsymbol{\varPsi }_{j}^{T}=\frac{2}{p_{n}^{\prime}\left( x_j \right)}\boldsymbol{b},
\label{eq4.9:energy}
\end{equation}
 Based on the fast Thomas algorithm applied to each system, the overall operation count is still $\mathcal{O} \left( n^2 \right)$.


Step-3: $W=\frac{1}{2}\varPsi S_n$, which also needs $\mathcal{O} \left( n^2 \right)$ operations since $S_n$ is a sparse matrix.


Thus, the matrix  $W=\varPhi ^{-1}$  can be computed with a complexity of  $\mathcal{O} \left( n^2 \right) $ .
\section{Numerical experiments}
\label{sec5}
In this section, we present a series of parallel experimental results corresponding to the second and third orders to verify the acceleration and parallel efficiency of the direct PinT algorithm proposed by us.In addition, we also provide a comparison of CPU time between our provided fast spectral decomposition of $B$ in the section 4 and the eig function in MATLAB. In addition, he previous experiment was completed using MATLAB2022a on a processor of Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-10200H CPU @ 2.40GHz   2.40 GHz and a machine - band RAM of 16GB and all experiments in Section 5.2 were conducted using MATLAB2022a on a processor Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14700K  3.40GHz   and a machine - band RAM of 32GB.
\subsection{Fast spectral decomposition of $B$}
In Section 4, we introduce  fast spectral decomposition of $B$  and the fast solution for  $V^{-1}$ , improving the parallel efficiency of step-(a) by explicitly constructing the matrix  $V^{-1}$ .
Let $B=V_{eig}D_{eig}V_{eig}^{-1}$ and $B=V_{fast}D_{fast}V_{fast}^{-1}$ are the spectral decompositions corresponding to the $eig$ function in MATLAB and our fast algorithm, respectively. Define the maximum relative difference as  
\begin{equation*}
	\eta _{fast\,\,}:=\frac{\left\| D_{eig}-D_{fast} \right\| _F}{\left\| D_{eig} \right\| _F}
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
	 w_{eig\,\,}:=\frac{\left\| B-V_{eig}D_{eig}V_{eig}^{-1} \right\| _F}{\left\| B \right\| _F},  w_{fast}\,\,:=\frac{\left\| B-V_{fast}D_{fast}V_{fast}^{-1} \right\| _F}{\left\| B \right\| _F}.
\end{equation*}

Table 1 shows the comparison of CPU time between using the $eig$ function in MATLAB and our proposed fast algorithm for spectral decomposition. The CPU time is estimated by the tic/toc function in MATLAB. The $Iter$ column represents the number of Newton iterations required to reach the tolerance  $tol=10^{-10}$ . The CPU time growth of our fast algorithm is much lower than that of the $eig$ function. Especially when  n = 8192 , the time required by the $eig$ function is approximately 10 times that of the fast algorithm. Considering the effects of discretization errors and rounding errors, \cref{tab:comparison} shows that due to the condition number of the eigenvector matrix  $V$ , the results of  $w_{fast}$  are not ideal. However, for $\eta _{fast}$ , the eigenvalues obtained by the two algorithms are basically the same.
