Hierarchical time series are collections of time series formed via aggregation, which satisfy some summing constraints.
Fig.~\ref{fig: simple hier} can represent, for example, the total visitors of a country (T), disaggregated by zones (A, B) and regions (AA, AB, BA, BB), so that:
\begin{equation*}
T = A+B, \quad A = AA+AB, \quad B=BA+BB.
\end{equation*}
%
At any time $t$, the hierarchical constraints between time series are given by: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq: u=Ab}
\ubf_t = \Abf \bbf_t,    
\end{equation}
where
$\mathbf{A} \in \rr^{n_u \times n_b}$ is the \textit{aggregation matrix}, which is made of 0 and 1 and specifies how the \textit{bottom} time series $\bbf_t\in \rr^{n_b}$ aggregate to the \textit{upper} time series $\ubf_t \in \rr^{n_u}$.
The \textit{summing matrix} $\Sbf \in \rr^{n \times n_b}$, defined as:
\begin{equation*}
    \Sbf = \begin{bmatrix}
    \Abf\\
    \mathbf{I}_{n_b \times n_b}
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation*} 
encodes the hierarchy via $\ybf_t = \Sbf \bbf_t$, 
where $\ybf_t = \left[\ubf_t^T, \bbf_t^T\right]^T \in \rr^n$.

We adopt the formulation based on the summation matrix, although all the reasoning extends to the more general linearly constrained time series framework introduced in \citet{Girolimetto2024}.

We consider probabilistic forecasts in the form of a joint predictive distribution $\pihat$ over $\rr^n$.
The forecast distribution $\pihat$ should be \textit{coherent}, i.e., it should give positive probability only to regions of points that satisfy the hierarchical constraints.
Hence, the support of $\pihat$ should lie on the \textit{coherent subspace} $\Scal$, defined as $\Scal := \{\ybf \in \rr^n \; \text{\small such that} \; \ybf = \Sbf \bbf \}$, so that any sample from $\pihat$ satisfies the hierarchical constraints.
The goal of probabilistic reconciliation is to find a coherent reconciled forecast distribution $\pitil$.
For a more detailed discussion on probabilistic reconciliation, we refer to \cite{panagiotelis2022reconc, zambon2024efficient}.

\paragraph{MinT reconciliation}

The Minimum Trace (MinT) reconciliation method was initially introduced for the reconciliation of point forecasts by \cite{wickramasuriya2019optimal}. 
Given the $h$-step ahead base point forecasts $\yH_{t+h|t}$, computed at time $t$ for time $t+h$, the reconciled point forecasts $\yT_{t+h|t}$ are obtained by projecting $\yH_{t+h|t}$ on the coherent subspace $\Scal$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq: minT point forecast}
\yT_{t+h|t} = \textbf{S} \Pbf_h \yH_{t+h|t},
\end{equation}
where $\Pbf_h = (\textbf{S}^T \Wbf_h^{-1} \textbf{S})^{-1} \textbf{S}^T \Wbf_h^{-1}$, and $\Wbf_h$ is the covariance matrix of the $h$-step ahead base forecast errors.
Assuming that the base forecasts $\yH_{t+h|t}$ are unbiased and that the matrix $\Wbf_h$ is known, 
the reconciled point forecasts $\yT_{t+h|t}$ are optimal in terms of the Mean Squared Error (MSE).

MinT was later extended to the probabilistic case by projecting on $\Scal$ the entire forecast distribution $\pihat$ \citep{panagiotelis2022reconc}.
If the joint base forecast distribution is Gaussian, i.e., $\pihat = \mvn(\yH_{t+h|t},\, \Wbf_h)$,
the reconciled distribution is also Gaussian:
\[
\pitil = \mvn(\yT_{t+h|t},\, \WT_h),
\]
where $\yT_{t+h|t}$ is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq: minT point forecast} and $\WT_h = \Sbf\Pbf_h\Wbf_h\Pbf_h^T\Sbf^T$.
In the Gaussian case, $\pitil$ is optimal with respect to the log-score \citep{wickramasuriya2024probabilistic}.


\paragraph{Probabilistic reconciliation via conditioning}
The reconciled distribution can be obtained by conditioning the joint forecast distribution on the hierarchy constraints \citep{corani_reconc_ecml, zambon2024efficient}.
Given \mbox{$\YH = \big[\UH^T,\; \BH^T \big]^T$}, 
a random vector distributed as the $h$-step ahead base forecast distribution $\pihat$; the reconciled distribution of the bottom series is given by 
\[\BT \;\;\sim\;\; \BH \;\Big|\; \big(\UH - \Abf\BH = 0 \big), \]
and can be extended to the entire hierarchy by pre-multiplying by $\Sbf$ \citep{zambon2024efficient}. 
If the base forecast distribution is jointly Gaussian, 
the solution of reconciliation via conditioning coincides with MinT \citep{corani_reconc_ecml, zambon2024probabilistic}.

\paragraph{Estimation of $\Wbf_1$}
For $h=1$, $\Wbf_1$ is typically estimated as the covariance matrix of the in-sample residuals. 
Empirically, the most effective choice \citep{wickramasuriya2019optimal, panagiotelis2021gemoetric, di2022forecast} appears to be the shrinkage estimator \citep{schafer2005shrinkage}, defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq: shrinkage}
    \SigmaH_{\text{shrink}} = (1 - \lambda_{\text{shrink}}) \WH + \lambda_{\text{shrink}} \widehat{\mathbf{D}},
\end{equation}
where $\WH$ is the sample covariance matrix, $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}$ is the diagonal matrix of the sample variances, and $\lambda_{\text{shrink}} \in [0,1]$ is the optimal shrinkage parameter.
We denote the MinT reconciliation method with the shrinkage estimator by \textit{MinT}.

\paragraph{Estimation of $\Wbf_h$}
There is no standard method for estimating $\Wbf_h$ for $h>1$.
\cite{wickramasuriya2019optimal} assume $\Wbf_h = k_h \Wbf_1$ for some constant $k_h$, which does not need to be estimated to compute the reconciled point forecast $\yT_{t+h|t}$.
Yet, in a probabilistic framework, $k_h$ is needed to compute the reconciled variance; some heuristic strategies have been proposed by \cite{corani_reconc_ecml}.
Alternative approaches are given by \cite{girolimetto2024cross}, where $\Wbf_h$ is estimated using multi-step residuals.
Our method can be applied with any choice of $W_h$;
in our experiments, we only consider the case $h=1$.
From now on, we drop the $h$ subscript for ease of notation.

\section{Probabilistic reconciliation with uncertain $\Wbf$}\label{sec: t-rec}

\paragraph{Prior of $\Wbf$}
We adopt an Inverse-Wishart ($\iw$) prior distribution for $\Wbf$.
%
Hence, we set
\begin{equation}\label{eq: prior on W}
\Wbf \;\sim\; \iw(\Psibf_0, \nu_0),
\end{equation}
where $\Psibf_0 \in \rr^{n \times n}$ is the scale parameter and $\nu_0 \in \rr$ is the degree of freedom.
The Inverse-Wishart is a distribution defined on the space of positive-definite matrices, with density 
\begin{equation*}
\iw(\Wbf;\, \Psibf_0, \nu_0) = 
\frac{|\Psibf_0|^{\frac{\nu_0}{2}}}{2^{\frac{n \nu_0}{2}} \Gamma_n\left(\frac{\nu_0}{2}\right)}  
|\Wbf|^{-\frac{\nu_0+n+1}{2}}
\exp\left(-\frac{\tr\left(\Psibf_0 \Wbf^{-1}\right)}{2}\right),
\end{equation*}
where $|\cdot|$ denotes the determinant, $\tr$ the trace, and $\Gamma_n$ the multivariate gamma function \citep{gupta2018matrix}.
%


\paragraph{Likelihood}
As in \cite{corani_reconc_ecml, wickramasuriya2024probabilistic}, 
we assume that, conditionally on $\Wbf$, the base predictive distribution is Gaussian with covariance matrix $\Wbf$, and that the base forecasts are unbiased.
The in-sample residuals $\rbf_1, \dots, \rbf_T$, defined as $\rbf_j = \yH_j - \ybf_j$ for $j=1,\dots,T$, are thus an IID sample from a multivariate Gaussian with zero mean and covariance matrix $\Wbf$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq: likelihood}
\rbf_1, \dots, \rbf_T \;|\; \Wbf \;\simIID\; \mvn(\bm{0}, \Wbf). 
\end{equation} 



\paragraph{Posterior of $\Wbf$} The posterior distribution of $\Wbf$, since
the IW is conjugate for the multivariate Gaussian likelihood \citep[Ch.~3.6]{bda2013}, is still Inverse-Wishart:
\begin{equation}\label{eq: posterior on W}
\pi\left(\Wbf\,|\,\Rbf\right) = \iw(\Wbf;\, \Psi', \nu'),
\end{equation}
with parameters $\nu' = \nu_0 + T$ and $\Psibf' = \Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T$,
where $\Rbf := \begin{bmatrix} \rbf_1 & \dots & \rbf_T \end{bmatrix} \in \rr^{n \times T}$ is the matrix of the in-sample residuals.
%
\paragraph{Multivariate t base predictive distribution}
We obtain the incoherent posterior predictive distribution of the base forecasts, by marginalizing out $\Wbf$; it
is a multivariate t-distribution \citep[Ch.~3.6]{bda2013}:
\begin{align}
\pihat\left(\ybf\,|\,\Rbf\right)
&= \int \pihat\left(\ybf, \Wbf\,|\, \Rbf\right) \; \, d\Wbf \nonumber\\
&= \int \pihat\left(\ybf\,|\,\Wbf\right) \; \pi\left(\Wbf\,|\,\Rbf\right) \, d\Wbf \nonumber\\
&= \int \mvn\left(\ybf;\,\yH,\,\Wbf\right) \; \iw(\Wbf;\, \Psi', \nu') \, d\Wbf \nonumber\\
&= \mt\left(\ybf;\, \yH,\, \frac{1}{\nu'-n+1} \Psibf',\, \nu'-n+1\right). \label{eq: posterior predictive mt}
\end{align}
%

\paragraph{Reconciliation}
The multivariate t-distribution is closed under affine transformations and conditioning \citep{Kotz_Nadarajah_2004}.
Hence, we obtain analytically the reconciled distribution via conditioning, as stated the following theorem, which we prove in \ref{app: rec via cond t}.

\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem: t reconc via conditioning}
Given a hierarchy specified by Eq.~\eqref{eq: u=Ab}, and
under the model of Eq.~\eqref{eq: likelihood} and Eq.~\eqref{eq: prior on W}, the reconciled distribution via conditioning of the bottom time series is a multivariate t-distribution:
\begin{equation} \label{eq: reconciled bottom distribution posterior}
\BT \;\sim\; \mt\left(\bT,\, \SigmaT_B,\, \nutil \right),
\end{equation}
%
where
\begin{align}
\bT &= \bH + \left({\Psibf'_{UB}}^T - \Psibf'_B \Abf^T\right) \Qbf^{-1} (\Abf \bH - \uH), \label{eq: reconciled t mean bottom} \\
\SigmaT_B &= 
C
\left[\Psibf'_B - \left({\Psibf'_{UB}}^T - \Psibf'_B \Abf^T\right) \Qbf^{-1} \left({\Psibf'_{UB}}^T - \Psibf'_B \Abf^T\right)^T \right], \label{eq: reconciled t variance bottom} \\
\nutil &= \nu' - n_b + 1, \label{eq: reconciled t nu bottom}
\end{align}
%
and 
\begin{align}
&C =  \frac{1 +  (\Abf \bH - \uH)^T \Qbf^{-1} (\Abf \bH - \uH)}{\nutil}, \nonumber \\  
&\Qbf = \Psi'_U - \Psibf'_{UB} \Abf^T - \Abf {\Psibf'_{UB}}^T + \Abf \Psibf'_B \Abf^T. \nonumber
\end{align}
\end{theorem}
%

Theorem~\ref{prop: t reconc via conditioning} provides the reconciled distribution for the bottom-level series. 
The reconciled distribution for the entire hierarchy,
obtained by pre-multiplying by the summing matrix $\Sbf$,  is a multivariate t with mean $\yT = \Sbf \bT$, scale matrix $\Sbf \SigmaT_B \Sbf^T$, and degrees of freedom $\nutil$.

\subsection{Parameters of the prior distribution}
\label{sec: prior_params}

We now show how to choose the scale matrix $\Psibf_0$ and the degree of freedom $\nu_0$ of the Inverse-Wishart prior.


\paragraph{Scale matrix}
$\Psibf_0 \in \rr^{n \times n}$ is a positive definite matrix, definded to be proportional to the expected value $\Psibf$ of the distribution via $\Psibf_0 = (\nu_0 - n - 1) \Psibf$.
We set $\Psibf$ as the shrinkage estimate of the covariance matrix of the residuals from a baseline method. For each time series, the baseline method is selected between the naive and the seasonal naive approach based on the outcome of the KPSS seasonality test, as implemented in the \texttt{forecast} package \citep{hyndman2008automatic}.

We empirically show in \ref{app:diagonal_prior} that using a diagonal scale matrix prior yields lower performances; 
this highlights that incorporating the full covariance structure is beneficial for improving reconciliation accuracy.


\paragraph{Degree of freedom}

We set $\nu_o \in \rr$, maximizing the model's out-of-sample predictive accuracy by using Bayesian leave-one-out (LOO) cross-validation \citep{vehtari2017practical}:

\begin{align}
\nu_o :&= \argmax_{\nu \in [n + 2, 5n]}\; \log\left(\prod_{i = 1}^T \pi(\mathbf{r}_i\,|\,\Rbf_{-i})\right) \nonumber \\
&= \argmax_{\nu \in [n + 2, 5n]}\; \sum_{i = 1}^T \log\left(\mt\Big(\mathbf{r}_i;\, 0,\, \frac{1}{\nu + T - n} (\Psibf_0 + \Rbf_{-i} \Rbf_{-i}^T),\, \nu + T - n\Big)\right), \label{eq: LOO}
\end{align}

where $\Rbf_{-i}\in \rr^{n \times (T - 1)}$ is the matrix of the in-sample residuals without the column $\rbf_i$,
and $\pi(\mathbf{r}_i\,|\,\Rbf_{-i})$ is the posterior predictive density of the residuals $\rbf_i$, given the observations $\Rbf_{-i}$.
Note that Eq.~\eqref{eq: LOO} follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq: posterior predictive mt}, with $\Rbf$ replaced by $\Rbf_{-i}$ and $T$ by $T-1$.
%
We restrict the search for the optimal value of $\nu_0$ to the interval $[n + 2, 5n]$, since the expected value of the IW prior is not defined for $\nu_0 < n + 2$. The upper bound $\nu_0 = 5n$ is sufficiently large such that, in our experiments, the optimal $\nu_0$ is consistently below it.

A naive evaluation of the objective function in Eq.~\eqref{eq: LOO} is computationally expensive as it requires the inversion of the $T$ matrices $\Psibf_0 + \Rbf_{-i} \Rbf_{-i}^T$, for $i=1,\dots,T$.
However, since these matrices are rank-1 updates of the same matrix:
$
\Psibf_0 + \Rbf_{-i} \Rbf_{-i}^T = \Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T - \rbf_i \rbf_i^T,
$
we use the Sherman-Morrison formula \citep{sherman1950adjustment} to obtain
\[
\left(\Psibf_0 + \Rbf_{-i} \Rbf_{-i}^T\right)^{-1}
= \left(\Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T\right)^{-1}
+ \left(1 + \rbf_i^T \left(\Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T\right)^{-1} \rbf_i\right)^{-1} \left(\Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T\right)^{-1} \rbf_i \rbf_i^T \left(\Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T\right)^{-1}.
\]
We thus compute only once the inverse of the matrix $\Psibf_0 + \Rbf \Rbf^T$, achieving a significant speed-up.
The optimization of $\nu$ is performed using the \texttt{nloptr} R package \citep{johnsonNLoptr}. 
In our experiments, in the worst case scenario ($n=111,\, T=60$), the optimization requires less than $0.5$ s on a standard laptop.


