We compare \textit{\tRec} and \textit{MinT} on the minimal hierarchy of Fig.~\ref{fig: min_hiearchy}.
In this setting the reconciled bottom and upper distribution are:
\begin{align}\label{eq: bot-up-t-stud-small-hierarchy}
    \BT \sim \mt\left(\bT,\, \SigmaT_B,\,\nutil\right), && \Util \sim t\left(\util,\, \sigmatil_u,\, \nutil\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
&\nutil = \nu_0 - 1, \notag \\
&\bT = \begin{bmatrix}
        \left( 1 - \frac{g_1}{Q}\right) \bhat_1 + \frac{g_1}{Q} \left(\uhat - \bhat_2\right) \\
        \left( 1 - \frac{g_2}{Q}\right) \bhat_2 + \frac{g_2}{Q} \left(\uhat - \bhat_1\right) 
    \end{bmatrix}, && \util = \Big( 1 - \frac{g_u}{Q}\Big)\, \uhat + \frac{g_u}{Q} \left(\bhat_1 + \bhat_2\right), \label{eq: u_tilde} \\
    \label{eq: sigma_u_tilde}
&\SigmaT_B = C \begin{bmatrix} \Psi_1' - \frac{g_1^2}{Q} & \Psi_{1,2}' - \frac{g_1 g_2}{Q}  \\ \Psi_{1,2}' - \frac{g_1 g_2}{Q} & \Psi_2' - \frac{g_2^2}{Q}
\end{bmatrix}, && \sigmatil_u^2 = C \left(\Psi_u' - \frac{g_u^2}{Q} \right),
\end{align}
and
\[
\begin{array}{l@{\hskip 6em}l}
C   = \dfrac{1}{\nutil} \left(1 + \dfrac{(\bhat_1 + \bhat_2 - \uhat)^2}{Q}\right), 
& Q = g_1 + g_2 + g_u, \\
g_1 = (\Psi_1' + \Psi'_{1,2}) - \Psi'_{u,1}, 
& \multirow{3}{*}{\(\Psibf_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 
\Psi'_u & \Psi'_{u,1} & \Psi'_{u,2} \\ 
\Psi'_{u,1} & \Psi'_1 & \Psi'_{1,2} \\ 
\Psi'_{u,2} & \Psi'_{1,2} & \Psi'_2 
\end{bmatrix}.\)} \\
g_2 = (\Psi_2' + \Psi'_{1,2}) - \Psi'_{u,2}, 
 \\
g_u = \Psi'_{u} - \Psi'_{u,1} - \Psi'_{u,2}, 
\end{array}
\]

The reconciled point forecasts (Eq.~\ref{eq: u_tilde}) are a combination of the base point forecasts;
for example, the reconciled upper mean $\util$ is a convex combination of 
$\uhat$ and $\bhat_1+\bhat_2$. This was already observed in the Gaussian case \citep{hollyman2021understanding, zambon2024properties}.

However, there is an important difference in the reconciled prediction intervals. 
In the Gaussian case, the width of the prediction intervals decreases after reconciliation 
since the reconciled variance is  smaller than the variance of the base forecasts \citep{zambon2024properties}.
%
In contrast, the prediction intervals of \textit{\tRec} can become wider than those of the base forecasts in case 
of large  incoherence; indeed,
the scale parameters in Eq.~\ref{eq: sigma_u_tilde} depend on the incoherence $\Abf \bH - \uhat$ through the term $C$.

\subsection{Simulations}

We compare the prediction intervals of \textit{MinT} and \textit{\tRec} using simulated time series.
We run 1000 simulations, in which we simulate the time series, compute the base forecasts, and reconcile them.
As in \cite{wickramasuriya2019optimal}, we simulate the two bottom series with a structural time series model with trend, seasonality and correlated errors.
The seasonality is set to $4$, and the training length to $12$. More details are reported in \ref{sec: simulation setup}.
We obtain the upper series  as the sum of the bottom series.
We introduce model misspecification by
computing the base forecasts with ets model with additive noise from the R package \texttt{forecast} \citep{hyndman2008automatic}.

We define the relative width as the ratio between the width of the reconciled prediction interval and that of the base forecast. In Fig.~\ref{fig:dist_PI_width_scatter}, we show  the distribution of the relative width of 
the 95\% prediction intervals of \textit{MinT} and \textit{\tRec}.
The prediction intervals of \textit{MinT} are always narrower that those of the base forecasts; 
indeed, the relative width is upper-bounded by 1.
On the other hand, the relative width of \textit{\tRec} can be also above one, and it is correlated to the incoherence of the base forecasts.

