\subsection{Single Regime Case}
To isolate the effects of stochastic resilience and liquidity, we first examine the single-regime case, where the Markov chain $I$ remains fixed in a single state. This is equivalent to setting the transition rate matrix $Q$ to zero, making each state absorbing. By doing so, we can analyze the impact of stochastic \textit{volume effect} independently from the influence of changing liquidity regimes. 

First, we examine how the value function responds to changes in various model parameters. The plots in Figure \ref{fig:value_function_variation} illustrate the impact of drift $h$, jump intensity $\lambda$, and volatility $\sigma$ on execution costs, where a lower volume effect corresponds to higher liquidity and improved execution. In particular, a higher volatility indicate greater market activity. The left hand side of Figure \ref{fig:value_function_variation} shows that a higher jump intensity leads to less favorable execution opportunities, thereby raising the overall execution cost. Likewise, increased volatility in the volume process reflects a dynamic market, helping to reduce execution costs. Figure \ref{fig:value_function_variation} also shows that higher resilience, which reflects a higher mean reversion speed, improves execution by accelerating liquidity replenishment. 
