Figure~\ref{fig:execise_continuation_vary_gamma} illustrates that when $\gamma_1 < \gamma_2$, the trader anticipates costlier execution in regime $2$ and adjusts their strategy accordingly. Indeed, in regime is $1$, where the price impact is lower than in regime $2$, an increase in the parameter $\gamma_2$ leads to an expansion of the exercise region (in regime $1$), as illustrated on the left-hand side of Figure \ref{fig:execise_continuation_vary_gamma}. Conversely, the right-hand side of Figure \ref{fig:execise_continuation_vary_gamma} shows that if we are in regime $2$, where the price impact is higher, the exercise region contracts as $\gamma_2$ increases. Consequently, the trader increases order sizes in regime $1$ to preempt higher execution costs associated with a potential switch to regime $2$, while in regime $2$, the trader decreases order sizes in anticipation of a favorable transition to regime $1$, where trading conditions are more advantageous.

