\section{Background} 

In this section we gather together the necessary background on Segal and Waldhausen's constructions of algebraic $K$-theory.  We assume that the reader is familiar with the basics of symmetric monoidal categories, and we write $\Sym$ for the category of symmetric monoidal categories and strong symmetric monoidal functors. Segal's group completion $K$-theory functor \cite{segal_categories_1974} $K^{S}\colon \Sym\to \Sp$ produces a connective spectrum from the data of a symmetric monoidal category. The underlying infinite loop space of $K^{S}(\cat C)$ is the group completion of the classifying space $B\cat C$. 

We will find it convenient to use \textit{permutative categories} (also called a strict monoidal category) rather than symmetric monoidal categories. Recall that 
a permutative category is a symmetric monoidal category for which the monoidal structure is strictly unital and strictly associative (see, e.g. \cite[Definition 4.1]{segal_e_1974} for a complete definition), and we write $\Perm$ for the category of small permutative categories and strictly unital strong symmetric monoidal functors.

\begin{notation}
    Let $\cat{C}$ be a permutative category.  For objects $C_1,\dots,C_n$ we write $T^n(C_1,\dots,C_n)$ for the product of the $C_i$.  Since the category is permutative, we do not need to specify an ordering of parentheses.
\end{notation}

Every symmetric monoidal category can be \emph{strictified} to an equivalent permutative category (\cite[XI.3 Theorem 1]{MacLane}, \cite[Proposition 4.2]{segal_e_1974}). Thus, virtually any construction which can be performed on permutative categories can also be applied to an arbitrary symmetric monoidal category by first applying strictification.

In \cite{ranicki_algebraic_1985}, Waldhausen develops a different $K$-theory construction that takes as input a different type of category, now called a Waldhausen category.

\begin{definition}
    A \textit{Waldhausen category} is a category $\cat C$ equipped with a zero object $*$ and two subcategories $c\cat C$ and $w\cat C$, called \textit{cofibrations} ($\cof$) and \textit{weak equivalences} ($\xrightarrow{\sim}$), satisfying the following conditions:\begin{itemize}
        \item[(i)] isomorphisms are both cofibrations and weak equivalences;
        \item[(ii)] for all objects $A\in \cat C$, the initial map $*\cof A$ is a cofibration;
        \item[(iii)] if $A\cof B$ is a cofibration and $A\to C$ is any morphism, the pushout $C\cup_A B$ exists and the induced map $C\cof C\cup_A B$ is a cofibration;
        \item[(iv)] gluing axiom: given a commutative diagram\[
        \begin{tikzcd}
            C \ar[d, swap, "\sim"] & A \ar[l] \ar[r, >->] \ar[d, "\sim"] & B \ar[d, "\sim"]\\
            C' & A' \ar[l] \ar[r, >->] & B'
        \end{tikzcd}
        \] the induced map $C\cup_A B \xrightarrow{\sim} C'\cup_{A'} B'$ is a weak equivalence.
    \end{itemize}
    An \textit{exact functor} between Waldhausen categories is a functor that preserves zero objects, cofibrations, weak equivalences, and pushouts. The category of (small) Waldhausen categories and exact functors is denoted $\Wald$. 
\end{definition}
Waldhausen defines a $K$-theory functor $K^W\colon \Wald\to \Sp$, which may be compared with Segal $K$-theory in certain cases. Every Waldhausen category has an underlying symmetric monoidal category, whose tensor product is given by taking pushouts over the zero object. However, not every symmetric monoidal category is a Waldhausen category, as not every symmetric monoidal category has coproducts.

\begin{definition}
    A \textit{Waldhausen category with choice of wedges} is the data of a Waldhausen category $\cat C$ along with, for all pairs of objects $X,Y\in \cat{C}$, a choice of pushout \[
    \begin{tikzcd}
        * \ar[r, >->] \ar[d, >->] & X\ar[d, >->] \\
        Y \ar[r, >->] & X\vee Y
    \end{tikzcd}
    \] so that $X\vee * = X$ and $*\vee X = X$. A morphism of Waldhausen categories with wedges is an exact functor that preserves choices of wedges, and we denote the corresponding category by $\Wald_{\vee}$.
    \end{definition}

In \cite[Proposition 8.8]{bohmann_multiplicative_2020}, Bohmann--Osorno show that the there is a categorically enriched (multi)functor $\Lambda \colon \Wald_{\vee}\to \Sym$ which sends $\cat C$ to a symmetric monoidal category whose structure $\vee\colon \cat C\times \cat C\to \cat C$ is determined by the choices of wedges.  By precomposing with strictification, we may freely assume that $\Lambda$ is a functor which takes values in $\Perm$.

In general, the Waldhausen $K$-theory of $\cat C$ may not agree with the Segal $K$-theory of the underlying symmetric monoidal category. A necessary condition is that every cofiber sequence in the Waldhausen category is split, at least up to weak equivalence.

\begin{definition} 
    A Waldhausen category $\cat C$ \textit{has weakly split cofibrations} if for every cofibration $A\cof B$ there is a chain of weak equivalences between $B$ and ${A\vee B/A}$, rel $A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[\S 1.8]{ranicki_algebraic_1985} and \cite[Theorem 10.2]{bohmann_multiplicative_2020}}]\label{theorem: weakly split K theory is the same}
    If $\cat C$ is a Waldhausen category with choices of wedges and weakly split cofibrations, then there is a natural equivalence $K^{S}(w\cat C) \xrightarrow{\sim} K^W(\cat C)$, where $w\cat C\subseteq \Lambda \cat C$ is the subcategory whose only morphisms are weak equivalences.
\end{theorem}

\section{Proofs of main results}\label{section:main}

In this section we prove the main results of the paper.  We begin by proving \cref{theorem: main theorem intro}, which is recalled below as \cref{thm:main}.  After that we recall Thomason's work on inverse $K$-theory for symmetric monoidal categories, and combine this with \cref{theorem: main theorem intro} to deduce \cref{introthm:inverse K}.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
    There is a functor $\Gamma\colon \Perm \to \Wald$ such that for any permutative category $\cat{C}$ there is a natural stable equivalence $K^S(\cat{C})\xrightarrow{\sim} K^W(\Gamma(\cat{C}))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{remark}
    If $\cat{C}$ is a symmetric monoidal category and $\widehat{\cat{C}}$ is its strictification, then there is a stable equivalence $K^{S}(\cat{C})\to K^S(\widehat{\cat{C}})$, thus \cref{thm:main} recovers the statement of \cref{theorem: main theorem intro} from the introduction.
\end{remark}

The construction of $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ addresses these issues by enlarging $\cat{C}$ so that, in effect, the monoidal structure on $\cat{C}$ becomes the coproduct.  This feat alone is not that difficult, but doing so in such a way that $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ admits a Waldhausen structure requires some care. In the end, the category $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ we construct remains permutative, and we show there is a natural chain of stable equivalences
\[
    K^W(\Gamma(\cat{C}))\xleftarrow{\sim} K^S(w\Gamma(\cat{C})) \xleftarrow{\sim} K^S(\cat{C}).
\]

\subsection{The Waldhausen category $\Gamma(\cat C)$}

We now proceed with the proof of \cref{thm:main}, beginning by defining the category $\Gamma(\cat{C})$. After possibly strictifying, we may assume that $\cat C$ is a permutative category, although we note this is purely a matter of technical convenience. For any non-negative integer $a$ we write $[a] = \{1,\dots,a\}$.  In particular $[0] = \emptyset$.  We write $2^{[a]}$ for the powet set of $[a]$.

\begin{definition}\label{definition: Gamma(X)}
    Let $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ be the category whose objects are tuples of elements $A = (A_1,\dots,A_a)$ in $\cat{C}$ for $a\geq 0$. A morphism $(\phi, f)\colon A\to B$ is given by the following data:\begin{itemize}
    \item a function $\phi\colon [a]\to 2^{[b]}$;
    \item for each $1\leq i\leq a$, with $\phi(i) = \{j_1\leq \dots\leq j_p\}\neq \emptyset$, a map $f_i\colon A_i\to T^p(B_{j_1}, \dots, B_{j_p})$ in $\cat{C}$.
\end{itemize}
For a morphism $\phi\colon [a]\to 2^{[b]}$ we will abuse notation and write $\im(\phi) := \cup_{i=1}^{a} \phi(a)$.  If either $a$ or $b$ is $0$ then there is a unique morphism $A\to B$. In particular, the empty tuple $()$ is a zero object.
\end{definition}
Note that there is no requirement that $\phi(i)\cap \phi(j) = \varnothing$ for $i\neq j$; however, we will add in this condition for weak equivalences and cofibrations.  The reason to not enforce this condition on all morphisms is that doing so would make it impossible to define pushouts along cofibrations.

\begin{example}\label{example: C into N(C)}
    Let $f\colon c\to d$ be a morphism in $\cat{C}$ and let $C = (c)$, $D = (d)$ be the corresponding objects in $\Gamma(\cat{C})$.  There is map $(\iota_1,f)\colon C\to D$ where $\iota_1\colon [1]\to 2^{[1]}$ is the map which picks out $[1]\in 2^{[1]}$ and $f$ is the map
    \[
        c \xrightarrow{f} d = T^1(d).
    \]
    After we define composition in $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ we will see that $\cat{C}$ is a subcategory of $\Gamma(\cat{C})$.  After defining weak equivalences we will see that $\cat{C}$ is a full subcategory of $w\Gamma(\cat C)$.
\end{example}

We need to define the identity morphisms and composition. The former is given by the ``identity'' $\iota_a\colon [a]\to 2^{[a]}$ (i.e. the map which sends $i\in [a]$ to the singleton $\{i\}$), and for every $1\leq i\leq a$, $f_i\colon A_i\to T^1(A_i)$ is the identity. Once we have defined composition, it will become clear that these morphisms indeed are identities.

\begin{definition}\label{defn:composition in Gamma}
The composition of $A\xrightarrow{(\phi, f)} B \xrightarrow{(\psi, g)} C$ is defined by the following data:\begin{itemize}
    \item the map $\psi\phi\colon [a] \to 2^{[c]}$ which sends $i\in [a]$ to $\cup_{j\in \phi(i)} \psi(j)$,
    \item for any $1\leq i \leq a$, we can write $\phi(i) = \{j_1\leq \dots \leq j_{p}\}$ and $\psi(j_{\ell}) = \{k^{\ell}_1\leq \dots\leq k^{\ell}_{q_{\ell}}\}$, so that $q_1 + \dots + q_{p} =q$ for $q=\abs{\psi\phi(i)}$. There is then a unique permutation 
    \[
    \sigma_{\psi\phi}\colon \coprod_{\ell=1}^{p} \psi(j_{\ell})\xrightarrow{\cong} (\psi\phi)(i)
    \]that orders the $k_i^{\ell}$'s according to the ordering of $[c]$. The $i$-th component of the composition is the composite
    % https://q.uiver.app/#
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	{A_i} & {T^p(B_{j_1}, \dots, B_{j_p})} \\
	& {T^{q}(C_{k^1_1}, \dots, C_{k_{q_p}^p})} & {T^q(C_{k_1},\dots, C_{k_{q}})}
	\arrow["{f_i}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow["{{T^p(g_{j_1},\dots, g_{j_p})}}"', from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow["\cong"', from=2-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow["{\sigma_{\psi\phi}}", from=2-2, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\end{itemize}
  \end{definition}
One can check that composition is strictly associative.  
\begin{example}
    If $c\xrightarrow{f} d\xrightarrow{g} e$ are composable arrows in $\cat{C}$ then the maps
    \[
        (c)\xrightarrow{(\iota_1,f)} (d) \xrightarrow{(\iota_1,g)} (e)
    \] 
    from \cref{example: C into N(C)} compose to $(\iota_1,g\circ f)$.  That is, the aisgnment $c\mapsto (c)$ is a functor $\cat{C}\to \Gamma(\cat{C})$.
\end{example}

We now define the Waldhausen structure on $\Gamma(\cat{C})$.

\begin{definition}\label{def:weak equiv}
    A map $(\phi, f)\colon A\to B$ in $\Gamma(\cat C)$ is a weak equivalence if $\phi(i) \cap \phi(j) = \varnothing$ for $i\neq j$ and for all $j\in [b]$ there is some, necessarily unique, $i\in [a]$ with $j\in \phi(i)$. That is, the sets $\phi(i)$ partition $[b]$. In particular, we can think of $\phi$ coming from a surjection $[b]\to [a]$ where $\phi(i)$ corresponds to the preimage of $i$.
\end{definition}

The description of composition above shows that weak equivalences are closed under composition; the essential point is that partitioning pieces of a partition yields a new partition.  

\begin{definition}\label{def: cofibration}
    A map $(\phi, f)\colon A\to B$ in $\Gamma(\cat C)$ is a \textit{cofibration} if it satisfies:\begin{itemize}
        \item $\phi$ factors as $[a]\hookrightarrow[b]\xrightarrow{\iota_b} 2^{[b]}$.  That is, $\phi(i)=\{j_i\}$ with $j_i\neq j_{k}$ for $i\neq k\in [a]$,
        \item for each $1\leq i\leq a$, the map $f_i\colon A_i\to B_{\phi(i)} = T^1(B_{\phi(i)})$ is an isomorphism.
    \end{itemize}
\end{definition}
\begin{example}
    The unique map $()\to A$ is a cofibration for any $A$.
\end{example}

To see that the cofibrations and weak equivalences defined above actually witness a Waldhausen structure we need to check that $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ has pushouts along cofibrations and the gluing axiom holds.  

\begin{definition}\label{defn: pushout}
    Given a map $A\xrightarrow{(\psi, g)} C$ and a cofibration $A\xrightarrow{(\phi, f)} B$, define an object $B\vee_A C =: D$ by the following data:\begin{itemize}
        \item the indexing set for $D$ is $[c]\amalg \{j\in [b]\mid j\not\in \im\phi \}$, ordered so that all the elements of $[c]$ come first. 
        \item for $1\leq i\leq c$ we define $D_i=C_i$ and for $i\notin \im\phi$ we define $D_i = B_i$.
        \end{itemize}
\end{definition}
We now show that this object is in fact the pushout in $\Gamma(\cat C)$. First note that there are canonical maps
    \[
        (\alpha,h)\colon C\to D\quad \mathrm{and}\quad (\beta,k)\colon B\to D
    \]
    given as follows.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The map $\alpha\colon [c]\to 2^{[d]}$ sends every $i\in [c]$ to the singleton $\{i\}$ (note that this factors through the injection $[c]\hookrightarrow [d]$). For  $1\leq i\leq c$ we then define $h_i\colon C_i\to T^1(D_i) =C_i$ to be the identity.
        \item The map $\beta\colon [b]\to 2^{[d]}$ is given by
    \[
        \beta(j) = \begin{cases}
            \{j\} & j\not\in \im\phi, \\
            \psi(i)\subseteq [c]  & \{j\} =\phi(i).
            \end{cases}
    \]
    For $1\leq j\leq b$ there are then two cases to consider when defining $k$ depending on whether or not $j\in \im \phi$. If $j\not\in \im\phi$, then $D_j = B_j$ so we let $k_j$ be the identity $B_j\to T^1(B_j)=B_j$.  If $\{j\}=\phi(i)$ then $\beta(j) = \psi(i)$.  If this set is empty we need not provide any new information.  If this set is non-empty, say $\beta(j)  = \{x_1,\dots,x_n\}$ we need to provide a map
    \[
        B_j\to T^n(C_{x_1},\dots,C_{x_n}),
    \]
    and we take this to be the composite
    \[
        B_j\cong A_{i}\xrightarrow{g_{i}} T^n(C_{x_1},\dots,C_{x_n})
    \]
    where the isomorphism is provided by the fact that $(\phi,f)\colon A\to B$ is a cofibration.

    \end{itemize}

    \begin{lemma}
        The map $(\alpha,h)\colon C\to D$ is a cofibration.
    \end{lemma}
    \begin{proof}
        We have already observed that the map $\alpha\colon [c]\to [d]$ is an inclusion and the maps $h_i$ are all identities, hence are all isomorphisms.
    \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
    Given a map $(\psi,g)\colon A\to C$ and a cofibration $(\phi,f)\colon A\cof B$ then $D$ is a pushout in the category $\Gamma(\cat C)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    A commuting square
    % https://q.uiver.app/#q=WzAsNCxbMCwwLCJBIl0sWzEsMCwiQyJdLFswLDEsIkIiXSxbMSwxLCJEJyJdLFsyLDMsIihcXGRlbHRhLFxcZWxsKSIsMl0sWzEsMywiKFxcZ2FtbWEscCkiXSxbMCwyLCIoXFxwaGksZikiLDIseyJzdHlsZSI6eyJ0YWlsIjp7Im5hbWUiOiJtb25vIn19fV0sWzAsMSwiKFxccHNpLGcpIl1d
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	A & C \\
	B & E
	\arrow["{(\psi,g)}", from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow["{(\phi,f)}"', tail, from=1-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow["{(\gamma,p)}", from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow["{(\delta,\ell)}"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
entails the following data. First, for all $i\in [a]$ with $\phi(i) = \{j\}$, there is an equality\[
\delta(j) =\bigcup_{j\in \phi(i)}\delta(j) = (\delta\phi)(i) = (\gamma\psi)(i) = \bigcup_{x\in \psi(i)} \gamma(x);
\] second, the diagram\[
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=huge]
    A_i \ar[r, "g_i"] \ar[d, swap, "f_i"] & T^{\abs{\psi(i)}}(C_{\psi(i)}) \ar[r, "{T^{\abs{\psi(i)}}(p_{\psi(i)})}"] & T^{\abs{\gamma\psi(i)}}(E_{\gamma\psi(i)}) \ar[d, equal] \\
    B_j \ar[rr, swap, "\ell_j"] &  & T^{\abs{\delta(j)}}(E_{\delta(j)}) 
\end{tikzcd}
\] 
commutes.

We need to show there is a unique map $(\omega,q)\colon D\to E$ making the usual diagram commute.  We are forced to define the map $\omega\colon [d]\to 2^{[e]}$ by \[
i\mapsto \left\{\begin{array}{cc}
    \gamma(i) & i\in [c], \\
    \delta(i) & i\not\in \im\phi.
\end{array}\right.
\] For each $i\in [d]$, we define $q_i\colon D_i\to T^{\abs{\omega(i)}}(E_{\omega(i)})$ to be either
\[
    D_i = C_i \xrightarrow{p_i} T^{\abs{\gamma(i)}}(E_{\gamma(i)}) \quad \text{ or } \quad D_i = B_i \xrightarrow{\ell_i} T^{\abs{\delta(i)}}(E_{\delta(i)}) 
\] 
depending on whether $i\in [c]$ or $i\not\in \im\phi$, respectively. We need to check that this makes the necessary diagram commute. We will check that \[
\begin{tikzcd}
     & D \ar[d, "{(\omega, q)}"] \\
    B \ar[r, swap, "{(\delta, \ell)}"] \ar[ur, "{(\beta, k)}"] & E
\end{tikzcd}
\] commutes, as the analogous claim involving $C$ is similar but easier. If $j\not\in \im\phi$, then \[(\omega\beta)(j) = \bigcup_{i\in \beta(j)} \omega(i) = \omega(j) = \delta(j),\] and on component maps we have \[
\begin{tikzcd}
  B_j  = D_j \xrightarrow{T^1(\ell_j)} T^{\abs{\delta(j)}}(E_{\delta(j)})
\end{tikzcd}
\] which is equal to $\ell_j$. Note that for $i\not\in \im\phi$, the fact that $k_j$ is an identity implies that the only way for this to commute is to have defined $q_j=\ell_j$.  Commutativity of the other triangle forces the choice of $q_i$ for $q\leq i \leq c$, hence the map $(\omega,q)$ is unique.

Now suppose $j=\phi(i)$ for $i\in [a]$. Then since the original diagram commutes, we have \[
(\omega\beta)(j) = \bigcup_{x\in \psi(i)} \gamma(x) = (\gamma\psi)(i) = (\delta\phi)(i) = \delta(j).
\] For $\delta(j)\neq \varnothing$, the composite \[
\begin{tikzcd}
  B_j \xrightarrow{\cong} A_{i} \xrightarrow{g_i} T^{\abs{\psi(i)}}(E_{\psi(i)}) \xrightarrow{T^{\abs{\psi(i)}}(p_{\psi(i)})} T^{\abs{(\gamma\psi)(i)}}(E_{\gamma\psi(i)})
\end{tikzcd}
\] is equal to $B_j \xrightarrow{\ell_j} T^{\abs{\delta\phi(i)}}(E_{\delta\phi(i)})$, as observed at the beginning of the proof.
\end{proof}
Since the map $()\to A$ is always a cofibration we see that we can form coproducts in $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ which are given by concatenation:
\[
    A\vee B := A\vee_{()}B = (A_1,\dots,A_a,B_1,\dots,B_b).
\]
Note that the canonical maps $A\to A\vee B$ and $B\to A\vee B$ are both cofibrations since we have a pushout of cofibrations
\[
    \begin{tikzcd}
        () \ar[r,>->] \ar[d,>->] & A \ar[d,>->]\\
        B \ar[r,>->] & A\vee B
    \end{tikzcd}.
\]
Moreover, note that the wedge construction is strictly associative, hence the category $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ is permutative.  It follows that the category $w\Gamma(\cat{C})$ is also permutative, with monoidal product given by taking wedges and the unit is the zero object.

\begin{lemma}
    The gluing axiom holds.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose we are given a diagram
    % https://q.uiver.app/#
\[\begin{tikzcd}
	C & A & B \\
	{C'} & {A'} & {B'}
	\arrow["{(\beta,g)}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
	\arrow["{(\phi,\ell)}", tail, from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-1]
	\arrow["{(\alpha,f)}"', from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow["{(\gamma,h)}"', from=1-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow["{(\psi,k)}"', tail, from=2-2, to=2-3]
	\arrow[from=2-2, to=2-1]
\end{tikzcd}\]
where the vertical maps are all weak equivalences.  We need to show that the induced map $(\mu,p)\colon B\vee_A C\to B'\vee_{A'} C'$ is a weak equivalence.  This is a condition entirely on
\[
    \mu \colon [c]\amalg (\im\phi)^c\to 2^{[c']\amalg (\im\psi)^c},
\]
where the superscript $c$ denotes the complement, namely that this map has image a partition of $[c']\amalg (\im\psi)^c$.  Unwinding the definitions one see that the map $\mu$ is the composite
\[
    [c]\amalg (\im\phi)^c\xrightarrow{\gamma\amalg \beta|_{(\im\phi)^c}} 2^{[c]}\amalg 2^{(\im\psi)^c}\hookrightarrow 2^{[c']\amalg (\im\psi)^c}
\]
and thus it suffices to show that $\gamma$ and $\beta|_{(\im\phi)^c}$ are both partitions.  For $\gamma$ this is immediate because $(\gamma,h)$ is a weak equivalence.  For $\beta|_{(\im\phi)^c}$ this follows because $(\beta,g)$ is a weak equivalence and $(\phi,\ell)$ and $(\psi,k)$ are cofibrations. 
\end{proof}

Finally, we show that cofibrations in $\Gamma(\cat C)$ are splittable up to weak equivalence. We first detail what cofibers look like.

\begin{example}
    Let $(\phi, f)\colon A\cof B$ be a cofibration, and recall that the cofiber $B/A$ is computed as the pushout of $()\leftarrow A\cof B$. By the description above, we see that this pushout is explicitly given by the data of:\begin{itemize}
        \item the indexing set $\{j\in [b]\mid j\not\in\im(\phi)\}$,
        \item for each $j\not\in\im(\phi)$, the object $(B/A)_j=B_j$.
    \end{itemize}
    In this sense, a cofibration is really just an inclusion of factors.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:weakly split cof}
    Given a cofibration $(\phi, f)\colon A\cof B$, there is a natural weak equivalence $(\psi,g)\colon A\vee B/A\to B$ (rel $A$).
\end{proposition}\begin{proof}
    Abusing notation, we write $\phi\colon [a]\to [b]$ for the induced injection.  Let $(\im\phi)^c = \{j_1,\dots,j_k\}$. The wedge is \[A\vee B/A = (A_1,\dots,A_a,B_{j_1},\dots,B_{j_k}).\] Let $\psi\colon [b]\to [a]\amalg (\im\phi)^c$ be the (unique) permutation that orders the image of $\phi$ first. The isomorphism $(A\vee B/A)_j\to B_j$ is the identity if $j\in (\im\phi)^c$ or $f_i$ if $j = \phi(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
    The definition of $\Gamma(\cat C)$ is reminiscent of the \textit{category of covers} associated to a category with covering families in \cite[Definition 2.12]{BGMMZ}, however, it is unclear how to compare the two in general and we do not pursue this idea further in this paper.
\end{remark}


\subsection{Comparing $K(w\Gamma(\cat C))$ and $K(\cat C)$}
Finally, we prove that there is a natural weak equivalence $K(\cat C)\xrightarrow{\sim} K(w\Gamma\cat C)$. This will be realized as the composite of maps  $K(\cat C)\to K(\cat C_+)\to K(w\Gamma(\cat C))$, where $\cat C_+$ is an auxiliary category that we now introduce.

\begin{definition}
    Let $\cat C$ be a permutative category. Define a new permutative category $\cat C_+$ with $\ob \cat C_+ = \ob \cat C \amalg \{*\}$ and $\hom\cat C_+ = \hom\cat C \amalg \{\id_*\}$. The permutative structure on $\cat C_+$ is given by the permutative structure on $\cat C$ but with $*$ behaving as the new (strict) unit, meaning $-\otimes * = \id = *\otimes -$.  
\end{definition}

Note that there is a homeomorphism $B(\cat C_+)\cong (B\cat C)_+$. Although the classifying spaces $B\cat{C}$ and $B(\cat{C}_+)$ are not equivalent, the group completions are.

\begin{lemma}[{\cite[Equation 1.6.3]{thomason_symmetric_1995}, see also \cite[Lemma A.1]{thomason_first_1982}}]\label{lemma: non unital equivalence}
    The (non-unital) strong symmetric monoidal inclusion $\cat C\hookrightarrow\cat C_+$ induces a weak equivalence $K^S(\cat C)\xrightarrow{\sim} K^S(\cat C_+)$.
\end{lemma}\begin{proof}
    Recall that an equivalent formula for $K^S$ is via group completion, i.e. $K^S(\cat C) \simeq (B\cat C)^{\rm gp}\simeq \Loop \mathbf{B}(B\cat C)$. Here $\mathbf{B}(B\cat C)$ denotes the bar construction on the $\EE_{\infty}$-algebra $B\cat C$, which can be modeled by taking the classifying space of the one-object topological category whose morphism space is $B\cat C$; we note that if $\cat C$ is permutative, then $B\cat C$ is a strict topological monoid \cite[Theorem 4.10]{segal_e_1974} and so this indeed defines a space of morphisms. We write $*_{B\cat C}$ for this one-object topological category, to distinguish it from the space $B\cat C$. 

    Now apply \cite[Proposition 3.8]{ebert_semisimplicial_2019} to the topological category $*_{B\cat C}$, considered as non-unital (which does not change the homotopy type of the classifying space), noting that $(*_{B\cat C})^+ = *_{B\cat C_+}$. This shows that $\mathbf{B}(B\cat C)\simeq \mathbf{B}(B\cat C_+)$, and the claim follows.
\end{proof}

There is an inclusion $\cat C_+\hookrightarrow \Gamma(\cat C)$ which sends $c\in \cat C$ to the singleton $(c)$ and $+\mapsto ()$; in the following proof, it will become clear that we need to add in $+$ so that something would map to $()$.

\begin{proposition}\label{proposition: Quillen theorem A}
    The inclusion $s\colon\cat{C}_+\to w\Gamma(\cat{C})$ is strictly unital oplax symmetric monoidal and induces a homotopy equivalence on classifying spaces.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    The functor $s$ is strictly unital by construction. To see $s$ is oplax, define structure maps
    \begin{equation}\label{equation: terminal object}
        (\phi,f)\colon(T^p(c_1,c_2,\dots,c_p))\to (c_1,\dots,c_p)
    \end{equation}
    in $w\Gamma(\cat{C})$.  We pick $\phi\colon [1]\to 2^{[p]}$ to be the map that picks out $[p]\in 2^{[p]}$, and let $f_1\colon T^p(c_1,c_2,\dots,c_p)\to T^p(c_1,c_2,\dots,c_p)$ be the identity.  This map is a weak equivalence since $[p]$ is a partition of $[p]$.

    To see that $s$ induces an equivalence on classifying spaces we apply Quillen Theorem A.  If $A=()$, then the fiber category $s\downarrow A$ has only one object, the identity $s(*)=()$. For $A\neq ()$, we will prove that the category $s\downarrow A$ is contractible by showing it has a terminal object given by
    \[
        s(T^a(A_1,\dots,A_a))\xrightarrow{(\phi,f)} A
    \]
    where $(\phi,f)$ is the same map as \eqref{equation: terminal object}.  To see this is a terminal object, suppose we are given any weak equivalence $(\psi,g)\colon s(X)\to A$.  Since the map $\psi\colon[1]\to 2^{[a]}$ must be $\psi(1) = [a]$, we see the only choice here is a map $g\colon X\to T^a(A_1,\dots,A_a)$.  So the data of a map $s(X)\to A$ is the same as the data of a map $s(X)\to s(T^a(A_1,\dots,A_a))$ over $X$, so this object is terminal.
\end{proof}

We note that an oplax symmetric monoidal functor induces an $\EE_{\infty}$-map on classifying spaces, and therefore a map on group completions. To see this, observe that if $F$ is oplax then the opposite map $F^{\op}\colon \cat{C}^{\op}\to \cat{D}^{\op}$ is lax monoidal and the composite   \[
        B\cat{C}\cong B\cat{C}^{\op}\xrightarrow{BF^{\op}}B\cat{D}^{\op}\cong B\cat{D}
\] is identical to the map $F$ and is a composite of $\EE_{\infty}$-maps. We have now assembled everything we need to prove the main theorem.  

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{thm:main}]
    We have shown that $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ is a Waldhausen category with choices of wedges and weakly split cofibrations.  Thus by \cref{theorem: weakly split K theory is the same} we have a weak equivalence $K^S(w\Gamma(\cat{C}))\to K^W(\Gamma(\cat{C}))$. \cref{proposition: Quillen theorem A,lemma: non unital equivalence} combine to give a map of $\EE_{\infty}$-spaces $B\cat{C}\to Bw\Gamma(\cat{C})$ which is a weak equivalence and thus group completes to a weak equivalence.  This yields a string of equivalences
    \[
        K^S(\cat{C})\xrightarrow{\sim} K^S(\cat{C}_+)\xrightarrow{\sim} K^S(w\Gamma(\cat{C}))\xrightarrow{\sim} K^W(\Gamma(\cat{C}))
    \]
    which proves the theorem. 
    \end{proof}

    Finally, we observe that the map $\cat{C}\to w\Gamma(\cat{C})$ is the unit of an adjunction; however, as this functor is the composite of a non-unital strong symmetric monoidal functor and a strictly unital oplax symmetric monoidal functor, we need to add more morphisms to $\Perm$. We therefore expand the domain of $\Gamma$ to be $\Perm_{\mathrm{nu}}^{\mathrm{oplax}}$, the category of permutative categories and non-unital oplax symmetric monoidal functors. 

    \begin{proposition}\label{prop: adjunction}
        The functor $\Gamma\colon \Perm_{\mathrm{nu}}^{\mathrm{oplax}}\to \Wald_{\vee}$ is left adjoint to the forgetful functor.
    \end{proposition}
    \begin{proof}
        We define the unit and counit of the adjunction, at which point verification of the triangle identities is straightforward. For $\cat{C}$ a permutative category, the unit is given by the inclusion $\cat{C}\to w\Gamma(\cat{C})$, which we have already observed in \cref{proposition: Quillen theorem A} is a non-unital oplax symmetric monoidal functor. For $\cat{D}\in \Wald_{\vee}$, we let the counit be the functor $\epsilon_{\cat{D}}\colon\Gamma(w\cat{D})\to \cat{D}$ 
        which sends the empty tuple $()$ to the zero object of $\cat{D}$ and a non-empty tuple of objects $(d_1,\dots,d_n)$ to the wedge $d_1\vee \dots\vee d_n\in \cat{D}$.  Given a morphism
        \[
            (\phi,f)\colon(d_1,\dots,d_n)\to (e_1,\dots,e_m)
        \]
        in $\Gamma(w\cat{D})$, the induced map $\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(\phi,f)$ is the composite
        \[
            \bigvee_{i=1}^n d_i\xrightarrow{\vee f_i} \bigvee_{i=1}^n \left(\bigvee_{j\in \phi(i)} e_j \right) \cong \bigvee_{j=1}^m \left(\bigvee_{\{i\mid j\in \phi(i)\}} e_j\right)\to \bigvee_{j=1}^m e_j
        \]
        where the last map is the fold map for each $j$; in the case that $\{i\mid j\in \phi(i)\}$ is empty we take the fold map to be the unique map form the zero object to $e_j$. Morphisms from/to the empty tuple are sent to the unique maps from/to the zero object in $\cat{D}$.

        To see that this functor is exact, we first note that it preserves the zero object by definition. Next, since each $f_i$ is in $w\cat{D}$, we observe that $\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(\phi,f)$ is a weak equivalence if $\phi$ is a partition, and hence this functor preserves weak equivalences. Preservation of cofibrations follows from the fact that in the inclusion of the zero object into any element in $\cat{D}$ is a cofibration.  
        It remains to check this functor preserves pushouts along cofibrations.  Given a span
        \begin{equation}\label{equation: span}
            (c_1,\dots,c_\ell)\xleftarrow{(\psi,g)} (d_1,\dots,d_n) \xrightarrow{(\phi,f)} (e_1,\dots,e_m)
        \end{equation}
        in $\Gamma(\cat{C})$ where the left leg is a cofibration.  Without loss of generality (i.e.\ up to isomorphism), we may assume that $\psi\colon [n]\to 2^{[\ell]}$ is induced by an inclusion $[n]\subset [\ell]$ with $i\mapsto i$.  We also assume that for $1\leq i\leq n$ that we have $c_i = d_i$ and $g_i$ is the identity. With these assumptions, $\epsilon_{\cat{D}}$ carries the morphism $(\psi,g)$ to the map
        \[
            d_1\vee\dots\vee d_n\hookrightarrow d_1\vee\dots\vee d_n\vee c_{n+1}\vee\dots\vee c_{\ell}
        \]
        which is the inclusion of a summand.  The pushout of the span \eqref{equation: span} is sent to
      % https://q.uiver.app/#
\[\begin{tikzcd}[]
	{\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(d_1,\dots,d_n)} && {\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(e_1,\dots,e_m)} \\
	{ \epsilon_{\cat{D}}(d_1,\dots,d_n)\vee (c_{n+1}\vee\dots\vee c_{\ell})} && {\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(e_1,\dots,e_m)\vee (c_{n+1}\vee\dots\vee c_{\ell})}
	\arrow["{\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(\phi,f)}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-3, to=2-3]
	\arrow["{\epsilon_{\cat{D}}(\phi,f)\vee \mathrm{id}}"', from=2-1, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
        where the unlabeled vertical morphisms are the canonical inclusions into a coproduct.  Such a diagram is always a pushout in a category with coproducts, and hence $\epsilon_{\cat{D}}$ preserves pushouts along cofibrations.
    \end{proof}


\subsection{Inverse Waldhausen $K$-theory}

\cref{thm:main} quickly implies one version of \cref{introthm:inverse K}.

\begin{theorem}
    For every connective spectrum $E$, there is a Waldhausen category $\cat C$ and a zig-zag of weak equivalences $K^W(\cat C)\leftrightarrow E$. 
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    In \cite[\S 5]{thomason_symmetric_1995}, Thomason shows that there is a functor $\Sp^{\geq 0}\to \Sym$ which is an inverse equivalence (after localization at stable equivalences) to $K^S$. We may post-compose with the functor $\Gamma\colon \Sym\to \Wald_{\vee}$ to obtain a functor $\Sp^{\geq 0}\to \Wald_{\vee}$, and by \cref{thm:main} this defines a homotopy inverse to $K^W$. In particular, this shows that $K^W$ is essentially surjective, and the localization claim follows immediately from the definition of the morphisms $W_K$.
\end{proof}
The Waldhausen category $\cat C$ in the theorem above is obtained by applying $\Gamma$ to the output of Thomason's inverse $K$-theory functor on $E$. However, there is another Waldhausen category $\cat N(X)$ defined in terms of retractive spaces over $X=\Loop^{\infty} E$ whose $K$-theory recovers $E$.
We detail this definition in the remainder of this section, as it is more in the spirit of Thomason's original work and in fact was the inspiration for the construction of $\Gamma$. We assume the reader is familiar with the language of operads and $\EE_{\infty}$-spaces, and the reader unfamiliar with these notions is referred to \cite{may_geometry_1972}.  For the remainder of the section, we fix an $\EE_{\infty}$-operad $\cO$ and when we say $\EE_{\infty}$-space we explicitly mean an $\cO$-algebra for this operad. We use $\Sp^{\geq 0}$ to denote a $1$-category of connective spectra, which may be modeled, for instance, by sequential spectra or grouplike $\mathbb{E}_{\infty}$-spaces.

We briefly summarize Thomason's argument from \cite{thomason_symmetric_1995}. The main construction is a functor\[
\Null/\colon \Sp^{\geq 0} \to \LaxSym
\] which associates every connective spectrum $E$ to a lax symmetric monoidal category $\Null/\Loop^{\infty}E$, recalled below in \cref{def:null X}. In \cite[\S 5]{thomason_symmetric_1995}, Thomason proves that the composite\[
\Sp^{\geq 0} \xrightarrow{\Null/} \LaxSym \xrightarrow{S} \Sym
\] is an inverse equivalence (after localization at stable equivalences) to $K^{S}$. 

A lax symmetric monoidal category is like a symmetric monoidal category, i.e.\ a category $\cat C$ equipped with an $n$-fold tensor product $T^n\colon \cat C^{\times n}\to \cat C$ for $n\geq 0$ (with $*\to \cat C$ picking out a distinguished object), except the associator and unitor transformations need not be invertible (see \cite[Definition 3.1.1]{leinster_higher_2004} for a complete description). 
Lax symmetric monoidal categories assemble into a $2$-category $\LaxSym$ whose $1$-cells are given by lax symmetric monoidal functors and whose $2$-cells are lax symmetric monoidal natural transformations (see \cite[Definitions 3.1.3 and 3.1.4]{leinster_higher_2004}). The inclusion of symmetric monoidal categories into lax ones gives a $2$-functor $U\colon \Sym\to \LaxSym$.

In \cite[Proposition 2.5]{thomason_symmetric_1995}, Thomason constructs a $2$-functor $
S\colon \LaxSym\to \Sym$ along with a 2-categorical natural transformation $\eta\colon S\circ U\Rightarrow 1_{\Sym}$. Additionally, he extends Segal's group completion functor $K^{S}\colon \Sym\to \Sp^{\geq 0}$ to apply to lax symmetric monoidal categories. In particular, there is a functor\[
Spt\colon \LaxSym\to \Sp^{\geq 0}
\] which, for $\cat C\in \LaxSym$, produces the group completion of the classifying space $B\cat C$. He produces a natural chain of weak equivalences between $Spt(\cat C)$ and $Spt(US\cat C)$, and for any symmetric monoidal category $\cat A$, there is a natural chain of weak equivalences between $Spt(U\cat A)$ and $K^{S}(\cat A)$. Consequently, for any $\cat C\in \LaxSym$, there is a natural chain of weak equivalences between $Spt(\cat C)$ and $K^{S}(S\cat C)$.

The missing piece of the argument is then to associate a connective spectrum $E$ to a lax symmetric monoidal category whose group completion $K$-theory recovers $E$. This is precisely the role fulfilled by $\Null/\Loop^{\infty} E$, whose definition we now recall.

\begin{definition}\label{def:null X}
    Let $X$ be an $\EE_{\infty}$-space and define $\Null/X$ to be the category whose objects are continuous maps $f_C\colon C\to X$, where $C$ is a weakly contractible space. We will abbreviate objects as simply $C$.  A morphism $g\colon C\to D$ is simply a continuous map such that $f_{D}\circ g = f_{C}$.
\end{definition}

In \cite[(4.5.2--4.5.5)]{thomason_symmetric_1995}, Thomason specifies a lax symmetric monoidal structure on $\Null/X$ by using the $\mathbb{E}_\infty$-structure on $X$. For $n\geq 1$, the $n$-fold tensor product $T^n(C_1, \dots, C_n)$ is given by\[
\mathcal O(n) \times C_1\times \dots \times C_n \xrightarrow{1\times f_1\times\dots \times f_{n}} \mathcal O(n) \times X \times \dots \times X \xrightarrow{\alpha_n} X,
\] where $\alpha_n$ is the $\mathcal{O}$-algebra structure map for $X$.  The map $\iota_C\colon C\to T^1(C)$ is the map
\[
    C\to \cO(1)\times C
\]
given by $c\mapsto (\mathrm{id},c)$ where $\mathrm{id}\in \cO(1)$ is the distinguished unit element.  The associativity and symmetry relations come for free from the corresponding relations for algebras over an operad. The consequence of Thomason's work is that there is a natural zig-zag of weak equivalences $X\leftrightarrow \Loop^{\infty} K^S (S\Null/X)$ when $X$ is grouplike, and therefore by \cref{thm:main} there is a natural zig-zag of weak equivalences $X\leftrightarrow \Loop^{\infty} K^W(\Gamma S(\Null/X))$.

We claim that there is another Waldhausen category $\cat N(X)$ which produces an equivalent $K$-theory spectrum to $\Gamma S(\Null/X)$, which is morally similar to the $\Gamma$-construction but more evidently related to $\Null/X$ and more explicit. In particular, the objects of $\cat N(X)$ are ``homotopically discrete'' retractive spaces over $X$, but the morphisms are quite different than just maps rel $X$.

\begin{definition}\label{definition: N(X)}
    Let $\cat N(X)$ be the category whose objects are retractive spaces 
    \[
        \begin{tikzcd}
            A\ar[r,shift left, "r_A"] & X\ar[l,shift left,"s_A"]
        \end{tikzcd}
    \]
    over $X$ of the form $A = X\amalg \coprod_{i=1}^a A_i$, where each $A_i$ is weakly contractible and the inclusion $X\to A$ factors through the identity on $X$.
    A morphism $(\phi, f)\colon A\to B$ is given by the following data:\begin{itemize}
    \item a map $\phi\colon [a]\to 2^{[b]}$ of finite sets, where $2^{[b]}$ is the power set of $[b]$;
    \item for each $1\leq i\leq a$, with $\phi(i) = \{j_1\leq \dots\leq j_p\}\neq \emptyset$, a map $f_i\colon A_i\to T^p(B_{j_1}, \dots, B_{j_p})$ such that \[
    \begin{tikzcd}
        A_i \ar[r, "f_i"] \ar[d, swap, "r_A"]& T^{p}(B_{j_1}, \dots, B_{j_{p}}) \ar[d, "{T^{p}(r_{B}, \dots, r_B)}"] \\
        X & T^{p}(X, \dots, X) \ar[l, "\alpha_{p}"]
    \end{tikzcd}
    \] commutes. We adopt the notation $T^{\phi(i)}(B_{\phi(i)}) := T^p(B_{j_1}, \dots, B_{j_p})$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Since every space in sight (except $X$) is weakly contractible, the maps $f_i$ are all weak homotopy equivalences.  Thus we think about the data of a map $A\to B$ as an explicit choice of how to ``replace'' some of the $B_i$ with some of the $A_i$, at least up to weak equivalence. The $j\in [b]$ so that $j\not\in \phi(i)$ for any $i$ correspond to the components $B_j$ that receive \textit{no map} from any $A_i$, and similarly the $i\in [a]$ with $\phi(i) = \varnothing$ correspond to the $A_i$ which are not mapped to any $B_j$. 

The idea essentially is that we can extend the definition of $\Gamma$ from \cref{definition: Gamma(X)} to take lax symmetric monoidal categories as input; we should think of an object $A\in \cat N(X)$ as defining the tuple $(A_i)_{i=1}^n$ in $\Gamma(\Null/X)$. All of the definitions (identities, composition, cofibrations, weak equivalences, and pushouts) work essentially the same way as in $\Gamma(\cat C)$, except they are slightly more complicated due to the lax structure. We detail how these definitions work for $\cat N(X)\cong \Gamma(\Null/X)$, although it is not difficult to see how one would extend the definition for arbitrary lax symmetric monoidal categories.

\begin{definition}
The identity maps in $\cat N(X)$ are given by $\iota_a\colon [a]\to 2^{[a]}$ and for every $1\leq i\leq a$, the map\[
(-,\id)\colon A_i \to T^1(A_i)=\cO(1)\times A_i,
\] which sends $a$ to $(a, \id)$. Composition is defined just as in \cref{defn:composition in Gamma}, except that checking that the result is actually another morphism in $\cat N(X)$ becomes a bit more involved (one needs to use the axioms of lax symmetric monoidal categories, the associative law for algebras over operads, and the symmetric equivariance of the operad action).
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
    There is a Waldhausen structure on $\cat N(X)$ given by:\begin{itemize}
        \item the zero object is $X$ itself;
        \item the weak equivalences are as in \cref{def:weak equiv};
        \item the cofibrations are as in \cref{def: cofibration}, except that the map $A_i\to T^1(B_{\phi(i)})$ factors as\[
        A_i \xrightarrow{f_i} B_{\phi(i)} \xrightarrow{(-,\id)} B_{\phi(i)}\times \mathcal O(1)
        \] where $f_i$ is a homeomorphism;
        \item pushouts are as in \cref{defn: pushout}, except that (for $j\not\in \im\phi$) $k_j=(-,\id)\colon B_j\to T^1(B_j)$.  
    \end{itemize}
\end{proposition}

Essentially the same proofs as the previous section work to show this indeed defines a Waldhausen structure, with the only additional complexity coming from keeping track of the additional coherence data in a lax monoidal category. Moreover, $\cat N(X)$ has a choice of wedges as before, with\[
A \cup_X B := X \amalg \coprod_{i=0}^a A_i \amalg \coprod_{j=0}^b B_j.
\] The same proof as in \cref{prop:weakly split cof} works to show that $\cat N(X)$ has weakly split cofibrations, and consequently $K^S(w\cat N(X))\xrightarrow{\sim} K^W(\cat N(X))$. 

Finally, the analogue of the inclusion $\cat C\hookrightarrow\Gamma(\cat C)$ is given by a lax symmetric monoidal functor $s\colon \Null/X\to w\cat N(X)$ which, on objects, is given by $s(C) = X\amalg C$, viewed as a retractive space using the given map $C\to X$. A morphism $h\colon C\to D$ is sent to the map \[
X\amalg C \xrightarrow{(\iota_1, h')} X\amalg D
\] supplied by the data of the identity on $X$, the inclusion $\iota_1\colon [1]\to 2^{[1]}$, and $C\xrightarrow{h} D \xrightarrow{\iota_D} D\times O(1)$. This definition is evidently functorial, and we can check that it is lax symmetric monoidal\[
s(T^n(C_1,\dots, C_n)) = X\amalg (C_1\times \dots \times C_n\times O(n)) \to X\amalg \coprod_{i=1}^n C_i  = s(C_1) \cup_X\dots \cup_X s(C_n)
\] via the map $!_n\colon [1] \to 2^{[n]}$ which sends $1$ to $[n]$, and the identity on $T^n(C_1,\dots, C_n)$. The definition on morphisms is similar. However, as before, this inclusion is not lax unital: the lax unit in $\Null/X$ is $x_0\to X$, where $x_0\in X$ is the image of the map $\mathcal O(0)\to X$, whereas the unit in $\cat N(X)$ is $X$. The same fix as before works, using the auxiliary category $(\Null/X)_+$, factoring the inclusion as $\Null/X\hookrightarrow (\Null/X)_+ \to w\cat N(X)$, and showing that the first map induces an equivalence after group completion and the second induces an equivalence on classifying spaces.

\begin{corollary}
    There is a functor $\cat N\colon \EE_{\infty}\text{-spaces}\to \Wald_{\vee}$ such that $K^W(\cat N(X))\simeq K^{S}(w\cat N(X))$ for all $X\in \Sp^{\geq 0}$.
\end{corollary}\begin{proof}
    We have shown that for all $\mathbb{E}_\infty$-spaces $X$, the category $\cat N(X)$ is in fact a Waldhausen category. Given $f\colon X\to Y$, we obtain an exact functor $\cat N(f)\colon \cat N(X)\to \cat N(Y)$ which is defined by sending $X\amalg \coprod_{i=1}^a A_i$ to $Y\amalg \coprod_{i=1}^a A_i$, where $A_i$ is a space over $Y$ via the composition $A_i\to X\xrightarrow{f} Y$. Since $f$ is a map of $\mathbb{E}_\infty$-spaces, the evident definition of $\cat N(f)$ on morphisms also makes sense and it is straightforward to check that $\cat N(f)$ is exact and that $\cat N(-)$ is functorial. The second claim follows from \cref{theorem: weakly split K theory is the same} and that the cofibrations in $\cat N(X)$ are splittable up to weak equivalence.
\end{proof}

We can now complete the proof of \cref{introthm:inverse K} as stated in the introduction. 

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{introthm:inverse K}]
Let $E$ be a connective spectrum and set $X= \Loop^{\infty} E$. By the corollary above, there is a natural weak equivalence $K^{S}(w\cat N(X))\xrightarrow{\sim} K^W(\cat N(X))$. On the other hand, the functor $s\colon \Null/X \to w\cat N(X)$ will induce an equivalence $Spt(Uw\cat N(X))\to Spt(\Null/X)$. By Thomason's theorem, we then have a natural zig-zag of weak equivalences\[
\Loop^{\infty}K^W(\cat N(X)) \xleftarrow{\sim} \Loop^{\infty} K^{S}(w\cat N(X)) \leftrightarrow Spt(Uw\cat N(X)) \xleftarrow{\sim} Spt(\Null/X) \leftrightarrow X. 
\] Therefore $E$ is isomorphic to $K^W(\cat N(\Loop^\infty E))$ in the stable homotopy category. This shows that $K^W$ is essentially surjective, and the localization claim follows immediately from the definition of the morphisms $W_K$.
\end{proof}

