% In CMBAgent, the interactions between different agents are achieved by a \texttt{ConversableAgent} framework. The human users who submit the initial request is represented by a \texttt{UserProxyAgent}. Each agent can receive messages from another agent, and operates based on a role-specific instructions set in the configuration files. The \textit{swarm} orchestration adopted by agent communication framework enables the system to solve the user request end-to-end without requiring intermediate human intervention. 

\item{\textbf{Role-specific agent instructions.}}
The behavior of each LLM agent is specified in a YAML-based configuration file. This file forms the main part of the agent’s system message and serves as the primary location for prompt engineering.

\item{\textbf{Context variable injection.}}
The system’s state is maintained through a set of context variables stored in a dictionary, which is shared among all agents. These context variables are updated via tool or function calls and injected into the relevant agent’s system message before the agent is invoked.

\item{\textbf{Structured output.}}
LLM responses can be structured using prompt engineering. Whenever feasible, we implement structured output by leveraging \textit{Pydantic}’s \texttt{BaseModel} classes. However, some models may be unstable or lack support for structured output. To address this, we introduce formatting agents whose role is to structure the response from the preceding agent. For formatting agents, we exclusively use OpenAI models, as all of them support structured output.


\item{\textbf{Tool calls.}} To operate on context variables, such as recording the execution status of a sub-task or tracking the number of steps in a proposed plan, we define functions that are registered with dedicated LLM agents. In our current implementation, each agent in \texttt{cmbagent} has at most one function registered. Moreover, when an agent has a registered function, we enforce the use of a tool call. It should be noted that tool calls, especially forced tool calls, are not supported by all models. Therefore, for all agents that perform tool calling, we exclusively use OpenAI models, which fully support both tool calling and forced tool calls.

\item{\textbf{Nested chats and history truncation.} Some of our agents trigger \texttt{nested chats}, of which only the output is kept, while other agents are operating on only the last $n$-messages of the (truncated) session chat. This allows for long sessions without reaching the LLM provider API token limits.}

\item{\textbf{Transitions.}} Transitions are managed through both function calls (return agent targets) and \texttt{hand\_offs}, using native \texttt{AG2} methods.
\end{itemize}
To develop \texttt{cmbagent}, we created a fork of the original \texttt{AG2} repository and adapted various parts to our specific needs. 
