\section{Simulated Matches}\label{sec:sim_matches}
%\subsection{Suspense and Surprise as a Function of Teams’ Scoring Rates}

To investigate how suspense and surprise are influenced by team strengths, we simulate matches between teams with varying scoring rates. We consider 1,326 unique scoring rate combinations, covering all pairings of \( \lambda_H \) and \( \lambda_A \) ranging from 0 and 5 in increments of 0.1.\footnote{Since football is a low-scoring sport, with an average scoring rate of approximately 1.5 goals per team per match \citep[e.g.,][]{Baker2018, Scarf2022}, a scoring rate of 5 represents an exceptionally high value.} For each pairing, we simulate 10,000 matches, resulting in a total of 13,260,000 hypothetical matches. The simulations assume no red cards and use historical minute-by-minute empirical goal distributions from the English Premier League to determine the minute-by-minute scoring rates.\footnote{The empirical goal distributions across the top 5 European leagues are similar, and we do not expect that selecting the empirical goal distribution of another top 5 league would significantly affect our results.} Subsequently, we calculate the suspense and surprise values for each simulated match, following the methodology outlined in Section \ref{sec:generalsetup}.

Figure \ref{fig:simulation_graph} presents the simulation results for the average level of suspense (Figure \ref{fig:sus}) and surprise (Figure \ref{fig:sur}) across different combinations of scoring rates, \( \lambda_H \) and \( \lambda_A \). Suspense is low (indicated in blue) when scoring rates are highly unequal, as one team is overwhelmingly dominant and almost certain to win the match. Conversely, suspense tends to be high (indicated in red) when scoring rates are equal, that is, when the match is perfectly balanced. However, suspense is zero when \( \lambda_H = \lambda_A = 0 \), since the outcome of a draw is certain, even though the match is perfectly competitively balanced. This illustrates the fundamental distinction between competitive balance and outcome uncertainty, as also noted by \citet{Scarf2019}.

