The paper is organized as follows. In Sec.~\ref{sec:model} we discuss how we extend Conway's GOL to the logistic GOL. In Sec.~\ref{sec:critical}, we report results from simulations of the logistic GOL and identify the different asymptotic dynamical regimes separated by critical points. In Sec.~\ref{sec:cluster_analysis} and Sec.~\ref{sec:cluster_size}, we characterize the critical points through extensive cluster analyses and power law distributions.
Lastly, in Sec.~\ref{sec:discussion} we provide a summary of the deterministic critical behavior identified here.


\section{Results}
\subsection{The Logistic Game of Life}\label{sec:model}

The original Conway's GOL is defined on a square lattice of sites, where each site goes through the parallel updating scheme:
\begin{equation}
    s_j^{t+1} = s_j^t + \Delta s_j, \label{eq: gol}
\end{equation}
where $s_j^t \in \{0,1\}$ corresponds to the state of $j^{\text{th}}$ site at time point $t$. $\Delta s_j$ denotes the quantity to be added to update the state from $s_j^t$ to $s_j^{t+1}$, and is a function of $s_j^t$ itself and the sum of states in its Moore neighborhood $m_j^t$ (\Fref{fig:model}a left panel), with $0\leq m_j^t \leq 8$. According to the finite-difference notation of Eq. \eqref{eq: gol}, a site in Conway's GOL can experience three possible updates: decay ($\Delta s_j = -s_j$ when $m_j<2$ or $m_j>3$), stability ($\Delta s_j = 0$ when $m_j=2$), or growth ($\Delta s_j = 1 - s_j$ when $m_j=3$) (\Fref{fig:model}a middle). 

