Third, we define \textit{clusters} to identify groups of equal-state sites that are connected through their direct nearest neighbors, i.e. up, down, left or right first neighbors. More precisely, a cluster $C^t$ denotes a set of sites in the lattice such that, for any two sites $j,j' \in C^t$, there exists at least one sequence $\{j,k,l,...,j'\}$ where
\begin{equation}
 s_j^t = s_k^t = s_l^t = \dots = s_{j'}^t,  
\end{equation}
and each site is direct nearest neighbor with at least one other site in $C^t$. Moreover, \( C^t \) is defined such that it is the maximal set of connected sites satisfying the clustering condition, and no subset \( \Bar{C}^t \subsetneq C^t \) is considered a separate cluster. Additionally, we define the size of a cluster $\cS^t$, denoting the number of sites in the cluster, i.e. the \textit{cardinality} of $C^t$:
\begin{equation}
    \cS^t = n(C^t).
\end{equation}
Finally, the cluster size ranking at a given time step $t$ is defined by the ordering $ \cS_1^t \geq \cS_2^t \geq \cS_3^t \geq \dots \geq \cS_i^t \geq \dots $, where the index $i$ here indicates the size rank of the cluster. The cluster sizes are then averaged over time and ensemble to obtain $\langle \cS_i \rangle$. 


