\subsubsection{Critical scaling of cluster sizes}

To further support the hypothesis that the transition at $\lambda_P$ is percolation-like, we investigate how the largest cluster's size $\expect{\cS_1}$ scales with the lattice size $N$ as we approach $\lambda_P$ (\Fref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}e), where we find that the scaling is the same as in classical percolation models~\cite{Stauffer1994,sahimi1994applications}. While relegating the methods to Appendix~\ref{sec: lambda_1 d_f analysis}, here we report the observed scaling relationships:
\begin{equation}
    \expect{\cS_1(N)} \sim
    \begin{cases}
        \log N, & \text{for } \lambda < \lambda_P, \\
        N^{d_f}, & \text{for } \lambda = \lambda_P, \\
        N^D, & \text{for } \lambda > \lambda_P,
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $D=2$ denotes the dimension of the system, and $d_f<D$ defines what is referred as the fractal dimension. In the `subcritical' regime ($\lambda < \lambda_P$), the largest cluster grows logarithmically with system size (left panel in \Fref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}e), meaning that there can be no giant cluster spanning the lattice. At the critical point ($\lambda = \lambda_P$), the largest cluster follows a fractal scaling, reflecting the self-similar nature of the percolating cluster (\Fref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}e middle panel). The fitted fractal dimension is \( d_f \approx 1.575 \) with a standard deviation of \( \sigma_f \approx 0.1 \), attributed to deviations from the exact critical point \( \lambda_P \) beyond five decimal precision, which also cause deviations in the large \( N \) regime. In the `supercritical' regime ($\lambda > \lambda_P$), the largest cluster grows with the system dimension ($D=2$), indicating the formation of a percolating cluster that spans the lattice (\Fref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}e right panel).   

Besides the scaling relations governing $\cS_1(N)$, the scalings of lower-ranked clusters $\expect{\cS_i(N)}$ indicate that, as $N\rightarrow\infty$, $\expect{\cS_i(N)}$ diverge to infinity only at the critical point $\lambda=\lambda_P$ (\Fref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}e). Note that the divergence of the second-largest peak is explicitly illustrated in \Fref{fig:SizeScaling} (see Appendix~\ref{Second largest divergence} for details). Taken together, all the analyses of largest clusters (Fig.~\ref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}) indicate the emergence of percolating cluster and a phase transition~\cite{newman2018networks} at $\lambda=\lambda_P$. 

% (\textbf{SMH: I think this part is a bit redundant now}) Overall, our findings demonstrate the intricate interplay between the deterministic control parameter $\lambda$, capacity dimensions, and cluster dynamics. At $\lambda_P$, we identify a point of self-similarity where all cluster shapes and configurations become indistinguishable across different grid sizes, providing evidence for the emergence of scale-invariance and power laws in cluster dynamics. In the next section we further quantify these power laws.

