\subsection{Cluster size distributions near the critical points}\label{sec:cluster_size}

Having previously established the scaling properties of the largest clusters with system size, we next investigate the extent to which cluster size \textit{distributions} near the critical points \(\lambda_P\) and \(\lambda_A\) follow power laws. To do this, we perform numerical simulations of the logistic GOL to compute the distribution of cluster sizes, $p(\cS)$, in the vicinity of each critical point (Fig.~\ref{fig:log_for_different_lambda}, Appendix). 


As a brief overview, at $\lambda_P=0.86055$, $p(\cS)$ seems to follow a power law, while for other nearby $\lambda$ values, distributions appear as stretched exponentials (Fig.~\ref{fig:log_for_different_lambda}a). On the other hand, there are multiple $\lambda$ values close to $\lambda_A=0.8750$ where the distributions are reminiscent of power laws, but only if the largest vacuum clusters are disregarded (Fig.~\ref{fig:log_for_different_lambda}b). While relegating technical aspects on computations of $p(\cS)$ to Appendix~\ref{sec:cluster size distributions}, below we leverage quantitative methods to test whether such distributions are indeed best described by power laws.


% Details on these numerical distributions, including trimming and probability density functions, are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:cluster size distributions}.  
The scaling of power-law data is rarely valid across the entire domain of cluster sizes. More often, the power law applies only for values greater than some lower bound $\cS_\mrm{min}$, i.e., only the `tail' follows a power law. In such cases, the cluster size distribution is expected to follow:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dicrete_power_law}
    p(\cS) = \frac{\cS^{-\tau}}{\zeta(\tau, \cS_\mrm{min})} \quad \text{for} \quad \cS \geq \cS_\mrm{min}
\end{equation}
where $\tau$ is the power-law exponent (the Fisher exponent \cite{fisher1969}), $\cS_\mrm{min}$ is the lower cutoff, and $\zeta(\tau, \cS_\mrm{min})$ denotes the generalized zeta function
\begin{equation}
    \zeta(\tau, \cS_\mrm{min}) = \sum_{\cS=\cS_\mrm{min}}^{\infty} \cS^{-\tau} = \sum_{\cS=0}^{\infty} (\cS+\cS_\mrm{min})^{-\tau}.
\end{equation}
The corresponding \textit{complementary cumulative distribution function} (cCDF) then reads:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{F}(\cS) = \sum_{\cS'=\cS}^{\infty} p(\cS') = \frac{\zeta(\tau, \cS)}{\zeta(\tau, \cS_\mrm{min})}.
\end{equation}
Using the numerically computed cCDF (Appendix~\ref{sec:cluster size distributions}), we determine the Fisher exponent $\tau$ and the lower bound $\cS_\mrm{min}$ by employing the Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) method \cite{Clauset2009, alstott_powerlaw_2014, barabasi2013network}. In addition to finding the optimal values of $\tau$ and $\cS_\mrm{min}$, the KS method assesses how well the power-law model fits to the data in comparison to other fat-tailed distributions. Details on the implementation of the KS method are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:KS method}.


%a method based on the KS statistic. Besides determining the optimal $\tau$ and $\cS_\mrm{min}$, the KS method also includes a plausibility test and a model comparison test. The first one returns a value ($p_{\mrm{gf}}$) to quantify how well the power-law model fits to the data, whereas the second test compares alternative fat-tailed distribution functions to the power law. Details on the implementation of the KS method are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:KS method}. 





Initially, we apply the KS method to the cluster size distributions for $\lambda$ values in the vicinity of $\lambda_P$. 
In this range, the logistic GOL unlocks dynamic control over cluster behavior, enabling precise tuning of the tail fatness in the cluster distribution through the tuning parameter $\lambda$. 
As shown in \Fref{fig:lambda_1_KS}a, the tail of the cCDFs undershoots the power-law line when $\lambda < \lambda_P$. As $\lambda$ increases, the number of zero clusters and the variance of cluster sizes increase, resulting in a fatter tail (\Fref{fig:lambda_1_KS}b). 
However, at $\lambda = \lambda_P$, we observe that the tail fits with a power law with exponential cutoff (\Fref{fig:lambda_1_KS}b). 
This cutoff is due to finite-size effects (\Fref{fig:SizeEvolutionofDists}). Further increases in $\lambda$ lead to the loss of perfect linearity of the cluster size distribution (\Fref{fig:lambda_1_KS}c), supporting the assertion that $\lambda_P$ is the critical point for the emergence of a giant cluster. 
Beyond this point, the largest vacuum cluster separates from the rest of the distribution and begins to percolate. 
As \(\cS_1\) grows to be comparable to the system size \(N^2\), it diverges from the main body of the distribution (arrow in \Fref{fig:lambda_1_KS}d). 
This evolution is also evident through simulation snapshots in \Fref{fig:FractalDim_NthLargest}a. 

The fit results of the KS method for cluster distribution at $\lambda=\lambda_P$ yield a power-law distribution with exponential cutoff, with the following coefficients:
\begin{align}
    \lambda_P &= 0.86055: \begin{cases}
        \tau = 1.81 \pm 0.03 \\
        \cS_\mrm{min} = 560 \pm 150
    \end{cases}  
    \label{lambda_1_fit_results}
\end{align}
The plausibility of the optimal power-law fit to the numerical data is confirmed by the KS method. Additionally, log-likelihood ratio tests whether alternative fat-tailed distributions (\textit{e.g.}, exponential, stretched exponential, or log-normal) offer a better fit than the power law. As shown in Table~\ref{table:statconclusion}, Appendix~\ref{model:goodness}, the power-law distribution with an exponential cutoff best characterizes the system at $\lambda_P$.



