The different natures of criticality at $\lambda_P$ and $\lambda_A$ points are also reflected in their Fisher exponents, $\tau$. At $\lambda=\lambda_P$, where $1 < \tau < 2$, all moments diverge, including the mean $\expect{\cS(\lambda_P)}$. This arises because the percolation behavior causes the bulk of the distribution to be highly concentrated in the tail. In the thermodynamic limit, the tail of the distribution (\Fref{fig:lambda_1_KS}c) extends to infinity, resulting in $\expect{\cS(\lambda_P)} \to \infty$. In contrast, at $\lambda=\lambda_A$, where $2 < \tau < 3$, the mean remains finite, and only the variance and higher moments diverge. This means that, unlike $\lambda_P$, the critical behavior at $\lambda_A$ does not constitute of clusters comparable to the system size. Below, we discuss on the potential mechanisms involved in the emergence of such power-law distributions.


\subsubsection{Contrasting mechanisms for criticality from cluster size distributions}

The mechanism behind the percolation transition can be explained as follows. As $\lambda$ approaches $\lambda_P$ from below, the system promotes more zero states because several neighborhood sums increase from $m\leq t_3$ to $m>t_3$, inducing decay instead of growth (Section~\ref{sec:critical}). As a consequence, clusters of quiescent states grow continuously with $\lambda$ until they merge with each other at $\lambda=\lambda_P$. In this respect, the dominance of zero states in the grid and the power law behavior of cluster sizes indicates that $\lambda_P$  marks the point of a deterministic percolation transition. We moreover note that the cluster size distribution exponent $\tau \simeq 1.81$ found in $\lambda_P$ is lower than exponents in classical 2D ordinary percolation models ($\tau>2$), hence the universality class of this transition remains unclear. However, we also note that extremely similar exponents have been observed in interesting scenarios, such as the no-enclave percolation model~\cite{Sheinman2015} and the percolation of sites not visited by a 2D random walk~\cite{Federbush2021}. 
% Moreover, we also note that the hyper-scaling relation $\tau = D/d_f+1$ holds in the deterministic percolation that we observe here.

The mechanism for the emergence of power law around $\lambda_A$ is fundamentally different from $\lambda_P$. Around $\lambda_A$, the system is dominated by a vacuum cluster of quiescent states that serves as a `playground' for activity with diverging susceptibility. This susceptible activity spreads in a particular fashion such that it `encircles' quiescent regions in the grid, giving rise to smaller zero-state clusters (Fig.~\ref{fig:SOC}). Interestingly, the size of these zero-state clusters encircled by activity follows a power-law distribution, which emerges close to $\lambda_A$. Beyond this value, the power-law ceases because the asymptotic activity stops due to more neighborhood sums inducing decay instead of growth.  

% As $\lambda$ approaches $\lambda_P$ from below, the system promotes more zero states because several neighborhood sums increase from $m<t_3$ to $m\geq t_3$, inducing decay instead of growth (Section SMH). As a consequence, zero-state clusters grow continuously with $\lambda$ until they merge with each other to form the giant cluster. In this case, several analyses identify a percolation-like phase transition. (The corresponding universality class is to be pursued in future studies.\textbf{ SMH: we should discuss this more thoroughly}) 

