A common method to compare models is the \textit{likelihood ratio test} -- to compute the likelihood of the data under two competing distributions, and take the logarithm of the ratio of the two likelihoods, denoted by $\mathcal{LR}$. \\

\begin{gather}
    \mathcal{R} = \frac{\mathcal{L}_1}{\mathcal{L}_2} = \prod_{i=1}^{n} \frac{p_1(\cS_i)}{p_2(\cS_i)} \notag \\
    \mathcal{LR} = \ln \mathcal{R} = \ln \mathcal{L}_1 - \ln \mathcal{L}_2
\end{gather}




If $\mathcal{LR}$ is positive, the first distribution is favored; if negative, the second distribution is favored; if close to zero, the data are insufficient to favor either model. We further apply the method proposed by Vuong \cite{vuong1989likelihood} which gives a $p$-value ($p_\textit{LR}$) that tells us whether the observed sign of $\mathcal{LR}$ is statistically significant. If this $p_\textit{LR}$-value is small (typically, $p_\textit{LR} < 0.05$), then the sign is a reliable indicator of which model is a better fit to the data. 





