{\em Betti numbers for two dimensions}: Let us consider two dimensional topological spaces that have orientable boundaries. There are two Betti numbers for such spaces, namely, $b_0$ which counts the number of connected components (or disjoint pieces), and $b_1$ which counts the number of one dimensional holes. The Euler characteristic, $\chi$, is given by the difference of the Betti numbers, as,
\be\chi=b_0-b_1.\ee
We also introduce the sum of Betti numbers
\be
b_{\rm sum}= b_0+b_1. 
\ee

Fig.~\ref{fig:2dspaces} shows examples of a disk denoted by $Q_0$ (left), an annular disk containing one hole denoted by $Q_1$ (middle), and a disk containing 2 holes denoted by $Q_2$  (right).  $Q_0$ has one positively (by convention) oriented boundary enclosing it. It has $b_0=1, \,b_1=0,\, \chi=1,\,\bs=1$. $Q_1$ has one positively oriented boundary and one negatively oriented one enclosing the hole. It has $b_0=1, \,b_1=1,\, \chi=0,\,\bs=2$. $Q_2$ has one positively oriented boundary and two negatively oriented ones enclosing the two holes. It has $b_0=1, \,b_1=2,\, \chi=-1,\,\bs=3$.  For our purpose here, topological spaces that have the same number of holes are not distinguishuable. 


